window.QUESTIONS.push(
Q(1, 'security', 'Zero Trust - assume breach',
  'A security team designs its environment on the assumption that an attacker may already be inside the network. Which Zero Trust principle does this reflect?',
  ['Assume breach', 'Verify explicitly', 'Least privilege', 'Defence in depth'],
  ['A'],
  'Assume breach means designing as if a compromise has already happened: segment workloads, monitor traffic, and limit how far an attacker can move. It is one of the three Zero Trust principles, alongside verify explicitly and least privilege.\n\n- **B** is about authenticating and authorising each request, not about assuming a foothold.\n- **C** is about granting only the access a task needs.\n- **D** is a layered-controls strategy, not a Zero Trust principle.\n\n**Exam tip:** "already inside" or "minimise blast radius" points to assume breach.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe Zero Trust', { diagram: 'zero-trust-flow' }),
Q(2, 'security', 'Zero Trust - verify explicitly',
  'Which Zero Trust principle requires every access request to be authenticated and authorised using all available signals?',
  ['Verify explicitly', 'Assume breach', 'Least privilege', 'Separation of duties'],
  ['A'],
  'Verify explicitly means always authenticate and authorise based on the identity, device, location, and data involved, rather than trusting a request because it comes from inside a network.\n\n- **B** is about designing for an existing compromise.\n- **C** limits the permissions granted, but does not describe the decision for each request.\n- **D** stops one person holding conflicting duties, which is a different control.\n\n**Exam tip:** verify explicitly is the "always authenticate and authorise" principle.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe Zero Trust'),
Q(3, 'security', 'Zero Trust - least privilege',
  'A helpdesk technician is given only the permission to reset passwords, with no other administrative rights. Which principle is being applied?',
  ['Least privilege', 'Assume breach', 'Defence in depth', 'Non-repudiation'],
  ['A'],
  'Least privilege grants the minimum access needed for the job and nothing more. Narrow roles, just-in-time elevation, and time-bound assignments all reduce the damage a compromised account can cause.\n\n- **B** is a design assumption about attackers already being present.\n- **C** layers multiple controls.\n- **D** makes actions attributable to a person.\n\n**Exam tip:** "just enough access" is least privilege; "just in time" is its companion control.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe Zero Trust'),
Q(4, 'security', 'Defence in depth',
  'What does defence in depth mean?',
  ['Several independent controls protect an asset, so one failure does not expose it', 'A single very strong firewall protects the whole network', 'Every user is given administrator rights to reduce support calls', 'Data is stored in one encrypted database'],
  ['A'],
  'Defence in depth layers controls - physical, identity, perimeter, network, compute, application, and data - so an attacker must defeat more than one before reaching the asset. If a layer fails, the next layer still applies.\n\n- **B** is a single point of failure.\n- **C** removes least privilege.\n- **D** protects one layer only.\n\n**Exam tip:** the seven layers are physical, identity, perimeter, network, compute, application, and data.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe defence in depth', { diagram: 'defence-in-depth' }),
Q(5, 'security', 'Defence in depth layers',
  'In defence in depth, which layer contains the information an attacker ultimately wants?',
  ['Data', 'Perimeter', 'Network', 'Physical'],
  ['A'],
  'The data layer is the innermost layer and the reason for every other control. Encryption, access control, classification, and backup protect data at rest and in use.\n\n- **B** filters traffic at the edge.\n- **C** segments and routes traffic.\n- **D** protects buildings, rooms, and hardware.\n\n**Exam tip:** data sits at the centre of the defence-in-depth model; everything else exists to protect it.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe defence in depth', { diagram: 'defence-in-depth' }),
Q(6, 'security', 'Defence in depth layers',
  'Badge readers, security guards, and locked server rooms belong to which layer of defence in depth?',
  ['Physical', 'Identity', 'Application', 'Data'],
  ['A'],
  'The physical layer covers buildings, rooms, racks, and hardware access - cameras, guards, locks, and badges. Cloud providers secure their datacenters; customers secure their own offices and devices.\n\n- **B** covers users, sign-in, and permissions.\n- **C** covers the software itself.\n- **D** covers the information and how it is protected.\n\n**Exam tip:** if the control touches a door, a lock, or a person on site, it is physical security.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe defence in depth'),
Q(7, 'security', 'CIA triad - confidentiality',
  'Encrypting patient records so that only authorised clinicians can read them supports which element of the CIA triad?',
  ['Confidentiality', 'Integrity', 'Availability', 'Non-repudiation'],
  ['A'],
  'Confidentiality keeps information from being disclosed to people who are not authorised to see it. Encryption, access control, and classification all serve confidentiality.\n\n- **B** is about data being accurate and unaltered.\n- **C** is about the service being reachable when needed.\n- **D** is about proving who did something.\n\n**Exam tip:** confidentiality equals "only the right people can read it".',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe the CIA triad'),
Q(8, 'security', 'CIA triad - integrity',
  'A hash comparison proves that a downloaded file was not altered in transit. Which element of the CIA triad does this protect?',
  ['Integrity', 'Confidentiality', 'Availability', 'Accounting'],
  ['A'],
  'Integrity means data is complete, accurate, and has not been changed by anyone who should not change it. Hashing, digital signatures, and version control support integrity.\n\n- **B** prevents unauthorised reading.\n- **C** keeps the service reachable.\n- **D** records who did what.\n\n**Exam tip:** hashing and signatures are the classic integrity controls.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe the CIA triad'),
Q(9, 'security', 'CIA triad - availability',
  'A service is deployed across three availability zones so it keeps running when one datacenter fails. Which element of the CIA triad does this support?',
  ['Availability', 'Confidentiality', 'Integrity', 'Non-repudiation'],
  ['A'],
  'Availability means authorised users can reach the service and its data when they need them. Redundancy, zones, backups, and denial-of-service protection all support availability.\n\n- **B** limits who can read data.\n- **C** keeps data unaltered.\n- **D** proves who performed an action.\n\n**Exam tip:** uptime and redundancy questions are availability questions.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe the CIA triad'),
Q(10, 'security', 'Authentication vs authorisation',
  'What is the difference between authentication and authorisation?',
  ['Authentication proves identity; authorisation decides what that identity may do', 'Authorisation proves identity; authentication grants permissions', 'Both terms mean the same thing', 'Authentication applies only to administrators'],
  ['A'],
  'Authentication verifies who or what is connecting - with a password, certificate, token, or biometric. Authorisation then checks permissions, such as a role assignment, to decide which actions are allowed.\n\n- **B** reverses the two terms.\n- **C** is false; they are separate steps.\n- **D** is false; every identity authenticates.\n\n**Exam tip:** authenticate first, then authorise. A failure in either one blocks access.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe authentication and authorisation'),
Q(11, 'security', 'Non-repudiation',
  'Digital signatures and tamper-evident audit logs make it difficult for a user to deny performing an action. Which security concept is this?',
  ['Non-repudiation', 'Confidentiality', 'Availability', 'Least privilege'],
  ['A'],
  'Non-repudiation provides proof of origin and integrity so a person cannot credibly deny an action. Digital signatures, signed audit trails, and reliable time stamps support it.\n\n- **B** prevents unauthorised disclosure.\n- **C** keeps services reachable.\n- **D** limits permissions.\n\n**Exam tip:** "cannot deny it later" equals non-repudiation.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe security concepts'),
Q(12, 'security', 'Encryption at rest and in transit',
  'A database is encrypted on disk and clients connect over TLS. Which statement is correct?',
  ['Encryption at rest protects stored data; encryption in transit protects data moving over the network', 'Both settings protect data only while it is being transmitted', 'Encryption at rest protects data only while it is being transmitted', 'TLS encrypts the database files on disk'],
  ['A'],
  'Data has two main states. At rest means stored on disk, in a backup, or in a database; in transit means moving across a network. Each state needs its own protection, and most regulations expect both.\n\n- **B** and **C** confuse the two states.\n- **D** is false; TLS protects the connection, not the files.\n\n**Exam tip:** at rest equals stored; in transit equals moving.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe encryption and hashing'),
Q(13, 'security', 'Hashing',
  'Which statement about hashing is correct?',
  ['It is a one-way function that produces a fixed-length value used to verify integrity', 'It is reversible with the correct key', 'It encrypts data so it can be decrypted later', 'It replaces the need for access control'],
  ['A'],
  'A hash function turns any input into a fixed-length digest. The same input always produces the same digest, but the digest cannot be reversed into the input, which makes hashing useful for integrity checks and password storage.\n\n- **B** describes encryption, not hashing.\n- **C** is also encryption.\n- **D** is false; hashing does not control access.\n\n**Exam tip:** hashing is one-way; encryption is reversible with a key.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe encryption and hashing'),
Q(14, 'security', 'PKI and certificates',
  'What does a digital certificate do in a public key infrastructure?',
  ['It binds a public key to an identity and is validated by a trusted certificate authority', 'It stores the private key on a public web server', 'It replaces multi-factor authentication', 'It hashes every file on a device'],
  ['A'],
  'A certificate authority signs a certificate that links a public key to a name or service. Clients trust the authority, so they can trust that the key belongs to the identity it claims, which is the basis of TLS and many authentication flows.\n\n- **B** is wrong; private keys must be protected, never published.\n- **C** is unrelated.\n- **D** describes file integrity monitoring.\n\n**Exam tip:** certificates establish trust in a key; the CA is the trust anchor.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe PKI and certificates'),
Q(15, 'security', 'Malware - worm',
  'A malicious program spreads itself from device to device across a network without any user action. What is it?',
  ['A worm', 'A trojan', 'A virus', 'Ransomware'],
  ['A'],
  'A worm self-replicates and spreads across networks by exploiting vulnerable services, without needing a user to open a file. Worms are why patching and network segmentation matter.\n\n- **B** disguises itself as legitimate software and needs a user to run it.\n- **C** attaches to a file or program and spreads when that file is opened.\n- **D** encrypts data and demands payment.\n\n**Exam tip:** self-spreading with no user action equals worm.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe threat types'),
Q(16, 'security', 'Phishing',
  'Employees receive email that appears to come from the chief executive and asks them to buy gift cards urgently. Which attack type is this?',
  ['Phishing', 'Distributed denial of service', 'Ransomware', 'Brute force'],
  ['A'],
  'Phishing uses a fraudulent message that impersonates a trusted person or brand to trick the recipient into acting. When it targets specific people with personal detail it is called spear phishing; when it uses text messages it is smishing.\n\n- **B** floods a service with traffic.\n- **C** encrypts files for extortion.\n- **D** guesses passwords repeatedly.\n\n**Exam tip:** urgency plus impersonation is a phishing hallmark.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe threat types'),
Q(17, 'security', 'Ransomware',
  'Files on a file server are encrypted and a message demands payment for the decryption key. Which attack is this?',
  ['Ransomware', 'Phishing', 'Denial of service', 'Privilege escalation'],
  ['A'],
  'Ransomware encrypts data, or steals it and threatens to publish it, then demands payment. Backups, least privilege, patching, and email filtering reduce the risk, and immutable backups reduce the impact.\n\n- **B** is the delivery method often used, not the payload.\n- **C** makes a service unavailable through traffic volume.\n- **D** raises the attacker level of access.\n\n**Exam tip:** encryption plus extortion equals ransomware - and backups are the recovery answer.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe threat types'),
Q(18, 'security', 'Denial of service',
  'A website is overwhelmed by traffic from thousands of compromised devices and becomes unreachable. Which attack is this?',
  ['Distributed denial of service', 'Ransomware', 'Credential stuffing', 'Phishing'],
  ['A'],
  'A distributed denial of service (DDoS) attack uses many sources - often a botnet - to exhaust a service and make it unavailable. Defences include traffic filtering, rate limiting, and cloud DDoS protection.\n\n- **B** encrypts data for extortion.\n- **C** reuses stolen credentials to sign in.\n- **D** tricks users into disclosing information.\n\n**Exam tip:** many sources plus unreachable service equals DDoS, an availability attack.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe threat types'),
Q(19, 'security', 'Password attacks',
  'Attackers take usernames and passwords leaked from an unrelated website and try them against many accounts. Which attack is this?',
  ['Credential stuffing', 'Brute force', 'Phishing', 'Privilege escalation'],
  ['A'],
  'Credential stuffing reuses real credentials stolen elsewhere, betting that people reuse passwords. It is a password attack, and it is why password reuse and missing MFA are so dangerous.\n\n- **B** tries many combinations against one account.\n- **C** tricks a user into revealing credentials.\n- **D** raises an existing account level of access.\n\n**Exam tip:** leaked list reused across sites equals credential stuffing; MFA breaks it.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe threat types'),
Q(20, 'security', 'Identity attacks',
  'An attacker with a standard user account exploits a misconfiguration to gain administrator rights and then moves to other systems. Which attack type is described?',
  ['Privilege escalation and lateral movement', 'Denial of service', 'Phishing', 'Data exfiltration'],
  ['A'],
  'Privilege escalation raises the attacker level of access, and lateral movement uses that access to reach more systems. Least privilege, privileged access management, segmentation, and monitoring limit both.\n\n- **B** attacks availability.\n- **C** tricks a person into acting.\n- **D** is the theft of data, which may follow but is not the described step.\n\n**Exam tip:** escalation is about more rights; lateral movement is about more machines.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe threat types'),
Q(21, 'security', 'Social engineering',
  'An attacker calls the service desk pretending to be an executive and asks for a password reset. Which term best describes this?',
  ['Social engineering', 'A zero-day exploit', 'A denial-of-service attack', 'A brute force attack'],
  ['A'],
  'Social engineering manipulates people into breaking procedures or revealing information. Pretexting, impersonation, and urgency are common techniques, and security awareness training plus identity verification at the service desk are the defences.\n\n- **B** exploits an unknown software flaw.\n- **C** overwhelms a service with traffic.\n- **D** guesses passwords systematically.\n\n**Exam tip:** if a human is being manipulated, the answer is social engineering.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe threat types'),
Q(22, 'security', 'Shared responsibility',
  'In a cloud environment, which security responsibility always remains with the customer?',
  ['Protecting data, identities, and access configuration', 'Patching the hypervisor', 'Securing the physical datacenter', 'Maintaining the network backbone'],
  ['A'],
  'The provider secures the platform, but the customer always owns its data, identities, and the way it configures access. Which additional layers the customer manages depends on the service model.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are provider responsibilities for cloud services.\n\n**Exam tip:** Microsoft secures the cloud; the customer secures what it puts in the cloud.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe the shared responsibility model'),
Q(23, 'security', 'Threat, vulnerability, and risk',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes threat, vulnerability, and risk?',
  ['A threat is a potential attacker or event, a vulnerability is a weakness, and risk is the likelihood and impact of exploitation', 'A threat is a weakness, a vulnerability is an attacker, and risk is a control', 'Risk is a weakness, a threat is a control, and a vulnerability is an event', 'All three terms mean the same thing'],
  ['A'],
  'A threat is something that could cause harm, such as an attacker or a flood. A vulnerability is a weakness that could be exploited. Risk combines the likelihood of the threat exploiting the vulnerability with the impact if it does.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** mix up the definitions.\n\n**Exam tip:** threat equals who or what; vulnerability equals the weakness; risk equals likelihood times impact.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe security concepts'),
Q(24, 'security', 'Defence in depth',
  'Which two practices strengthen a defence-in-depth strategy? (Select two.)',
  ['Segmenting networks so a compromise cannot spread freely', 'Applying least privilege to every account', 'Relying on a single perimeter firewall', 'Disabling audit logging to reduce noise'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defence in depth assumes one control will fail, so it adds independent layers. Segmentation limits lateral movement, and least privilege limits what a compromised account can do.\n\n- **C** creates a single point of failure and is the opposite of layered defence.\n- **D** removes the evidence needed to detect and investigate an attack.\n\n**Exam tip:** layered, independent controls beat one strong control.',
  'Describe security, compliance, and identity concepts - Describe defence in depth')
,
Q(25, 'identity', 'Microsoft Entra ID',
  'What is the primary purpose of Microsoft Entra ID?',
  ['Cloud identity and access management for users, groups, and applications', 'Hosting virtual machines and storage', 'Filtering inbound network traffic', 'Managing device drivers'],
  ['A'],
  'Entra ID is the cloud directory and identity provider. It stores users, groups, and applications, authenticates sign-ins, and issues tokens that authorise access to Microsoft 365, Azure, and thousands of integrated apps.\n\n- **B** is Azure compute and storage.\n- **C** is a network security group or firewall.\n- **D** is operating-system management.\n\n**Exam tip:** Entra ID is the identity provider; Azure RBAC then decides what an identity can do to Azure resources.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(26, 'identity', 'Tenants',
  'A company subscribes to Microsoft 365 and receives a dedicated directory at contoso.onmicrosoft.com. What is this directory called?',
  ['A tenant', 'A subscription', 'A resource group', 'A forest trust'],
  ['A'],
  'A tenant is a dedicated instance of Microsoft Entra ID that represents one organisation. It holds the organisation users, groups, applications, and policies, and it can span many subscriptions.\n\n- **B** is a billing and access boundary for Azure resources.\n- **C** groups related Azure resources.\n- **D** is an on-premises Active Directory concept.\n\n**Exam tip:** one tenant can contain many subscriptions; the tenant is the identity boundary.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(27, 'identity', 'Users and groups',
  'Why assign permissions to a group instead of to individual users?',
  ['One assignment covers every member, which is easier to manage and review', 'Groups bypass the need for authentication', 'Group membership removes the need for MFA', 'Groups give every member the Global Administrator role'],
  ['A'],
  'Group-based assignment reduces administrative effort and error: add or remove a person from the group and their access follows. It also makes access reviews and auditing simpler because there is one assignment to check.\n\n- **B** is false; members still authenticate.\n- **C** is false; policies still apply.\n- **D** is false and would violate least privilege.\n\n**Exam tip:** assign to groups, review the group, and keep individual assignments exceptional.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(28, 'identity', 'External identities - B2B',
  'Partners must access a shared Teams channel using their own work accounts. Which Entra capability should be used?',
  ['B2B collaboration with guest users', 'B2C consumer identities', 'Managed identities', 'Conditional Access only'],
  ['A'],
  'B2B collaboration invites external users into the tenant as guests. They authenticate with their home organisation credentials, and the host organisation controls what guests can access through policies and access reviews.\n\n- **B** is for customers using your applications, not partner collaboration.\n- **C** is for Azure workloads, not people.\n- **D** is a policy engine, not an invitation mechanism.\n\n**Exam tip:** partners equal B2B guests; customers equal External ID (B2C).',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe external identities'),
Q(29, 'identity', 'External identities - B2C',
  'A retailer wants customers to sign in to its public app with a social account or a new local account. Which capability fits?',
  ['Microsoft Entra External ID for customers', 'B2B collaboration', 'Privileged Identity Management', 'Microsoft Entra Connect'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft Entra External ID (the successor to Azure AD B2C) provides consumer identity for public-facing applications, with local accounts and social providers such as Google or Facebook, branded sign-in, and separate user directories.\n\n- **B** invites partners as guests into the workforce tenant.\n- **C** manages privileged administrator roles.\n- **D** synchronises an on-premises directory.\n\n**Exam tip:** customers and consumers equal External ID; business partners equal B2B.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe external identities'),
Q(30, 'identity', 'Group types',
  'Which group type provides a shared mailbox, SharePoint site, and Teams team for its members?',
  ['A Microsoft 365 group', 'A security group', 'A mail-enabled security group', 'An administrative unit'],
  ['A'],
  'A Microsoft 365 group is a collaboration group: creating one provisions a shared mailbox, calendar, SharePoint site, and Teams team, and membership controls access to all of them. Security groups are for permissions only.\n\n- **B** assigns access to resources but has no collaboration workload.\n- **C** adds mail distribution to a security group but still no SharePoint or Teams.\n- **D** scopes administration, not collaboration.\n\n**Exam tip:** collaboration workloads equal Microsoft 365 group; permissions only equal security group.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(31, 'identity', 'Dynamic groups',
  'A group must automatically contain every user whose department attribute is Sales. What should be configured?',
  ['A dynamic membership rule', 'A static assigned membership list', 'An access review', 'A Conditional Access policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Dynamic groups evaluate a rule against user or device attributes such as department, job title, or country. Membership updates automatically as attributes change, which avoids manual list maintenance.\n\n- **B** requires someone to add and remove members by hand.\n- **C** recertifies existing access rather than building membership.\n- **D** controls sign-in conditions.\n\n**Exam tip:** attribute-based membership equals a dynamic group; it requires Microsoft Entra ID P1.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Microsoft Entra ID'),
Q(32, 'identity', 'Authentication methods',
  'Which authentication method is based on something the user possesses?',
  ['A code generated by an authenticator app', 'A password memorised by the user', 'A fingerprint', 'A username'],
  ['A'],
  'Authentication factors fall into three types: something you know, something you have, and something you are. An authenticator app or a security key is something you have.\n\n- **B** is something you know.\n- **C** is something you are (biometrics).\n- **D** is an identifier, not a factor.\n\n**Exam tip:** MFA needs two factors from different categories, not two passwords.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(33, 'identity', 'Multi-factor authentication',
  'What does multi-factor authentication require?',
  ['Two or more different factor types, such as a password plus an app code', 'Two passwords from different systems', 'A username and an email address', 'One very long password'],
  ['A'],
  'MFA combines factors from different categories - know, have, are - so a stolen password alone is not enough. Common pairs are password plus authenticator code, or passwordless phone sign-in with a biometric.\n\n- **B** is two instances of the same factor.\n- **C** are identifiers, not factors.\n- **D** is still a single factor.\n\n**Exam tip:** different categories, not different values.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(34, 'identity', 'Passwordless authentication',
  'What does passwordless authentication allow a user to do?',
  ['Sign in without a password using a security key, an app, or a biometric', 'Sign in with a shorter password', 'Skip authentication entirely', 'Share one password across a team'],
  ['A'],
  'Passwordless methods replace the password with a stronger credential: Windows Hello for Business, the Microsoft Authenticator app with phone sign-in, or a FIDO2 security key. The credential is bound to a device or key, which also resists phishing.\n\n- **B** still uses a password.\n- **C** would remove security.\n- **D** violates accountability.\n\n**Exam tip:** passwordless is about removing the password, not shortening it.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(35, 'identity', 'FIDO2 security keys',
  'Which statement about FIDO2 security keys is correct?',
  ['They are hardware authenticators that resist phishing because they are bound to the website origin', 'They store passwords in a browser', 'They work only with on-premises Active Directory', 'They replace the need for a username'],
  ['A'],
  'A FIDO2 key performs a cryptographic challenge bound to the relying party origin, so a fake site cannot reuse the response. Keys are portable, work across devices, and can serve as a primary or second factor.\n\n- **B** is a password manager, not FIDO2.\n- **C** is false; FIDO2 is a web standard used with Entra ID.\n- **D** is false; the key still belongs to an account.\n\n**Exam tip:** FIDO2 and Windows Hello for Business are the phishing-resistant options.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(36, 'identity', 'Windows Hello for Business',
  'How does Windows Hello for Business authenticate a user?',
  ['With a PIN or biometric bound to the specific device and protected by its hardware', 'With a password typed on any shared computer', 'With an SMS code sent to a phone', 'With a certificate stored on a network share'],
  ['A'],
  'Windows Hello for Business creates a device-bound key protected by the TPM and unlocks it with a PIN or biometric. The PIN is not a password: it never leaves the device and cannot be reused on another machine, which makes the method phishing-resistant.\n\n- **B** is the password model it replaces.\n- **C** is SMS-based MFA.\n- **D** would expose the private key.\n\n**Exam tip:** device-bound plus TPM plus biometric or PIN equals Windows Hello for Business.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(37, 'identity', 'Self-service password reset',
  'What does self-service password reset let users do?',
  ['Reset or unlock their own password after proving their identity with registered methods', 'Reset any other user password without permission', 'Disable MFA for the tenant', 'Create new administrator accounts'],
  ['A'],
  'SSPR lets users recover access without calling the service desk, using registered verification methods such as an authenticator app, phone, or email. With password writeback, the new password is also written to on-premises Active Directory.\n\n- **B** would be a serious security hole.\n- **C** is an authentication policy decision.\n- **D** requires an administrator role.\n\n**Exam tip:** SSPR reduces helpdesk cost; password writeback extends it to hybrid identities.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(38, 'identity', 'Password protection',
  'Which Entra feature blocks users from choosing passwords such as "Password123" or the company name?',
  ['Microsoft Entra Password Protection', 'Conditional Access', 'Privileged Identity Management', 'Access reviews'],
  ['A'],
  'Password Protection compares new passwords against a global banned list maintained by Microsoft and an optional custom banned list of terms specific to the organisation, such as brand names or locations.\n\n- **B** evaluates sign-in conditions.\n- **C** manages privileged roles.\n- **D** recertifies access.\n\n**Exam tip:** weak-password blocking is Password Protection, not MFA and not Conditional Access.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(39, 'identity', 'Conditional Access',
  'What is the purpose of Microsoft Entra Conditional Access?',
  ['To evaluate sign-in signals and apply an allow, block, or challenge decision', 'To assign Azure resource permissions', 'To synchronise users from Active Directory', 'To store passwords securely'],
  ['A'],
  'Conditional Access is an if-then policy engine. It checks signals such as user, group, application, location, device state, and risk, then enforces a control such as require MFA, require a compliant device, or block access.\n\n- **B** is Azure RBAC.\n- **C** is Microsoft Entra Connect.\n- **D** is credential storage, not a policy engine.\n\n**Exam tip:** Conditional Access decides how a sign-in may proceed; it does not grant resource permissions.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Conditional Access'),
Q(40, 'identity', 'Conditional Access signals',
  'Which items does a Conditional Access policy evaluate? (Select two.)',
  ['The user or group and the target application', 'The device state and the sign-in location', 'The colour of the resource group tag', 'The number of questions in an exam'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Assignments decide who and what a policy applies to, and conditions add context: location, device platform and compliance, client app, and sign-in risk. Together they drive the access decision.\n\n- **C** and **D** have no security meaning and are not signals.\n\n**Exam tip:** think who, what, and where - then the control.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Conditional Access'),
Q(41, 'identity', 'Conditional Access controls',
  'A policy must allow access only from devices managed by the organisation. Which grant control should be used?',
  ['Require the device to be compliant or hybrid joined', 'Require a longer password', 'Block all browser access', 'Require an access review'],
  ['A'],
  'Device-state grant controls let access continue only when the device is compliant with policy or hybrid joined to the domain. Combined with app protection policies, they also protect corporate data on mobile devices.\n\n- **B** does not prove device management.\n- **C** would break legitimate access.\n- **D** is a governance task, not a sign-in control.\n\n**Exam tip:** managed-device requirements are Conditional Access grant controls.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Conditional Access'),
Q(42, 'identity', 'Identity Protection - user risk',
  'What does a high user risk level in Microsoft Entra ID Protection indicate?',
  ['The user credentials are likely compromised', 'The user has too many group memberships', 'The user password is about to expire', 'The user is signing in from a managed device'],
  ['A'],
  'User risk represents the probability that an account is compromised, based on signals such as leaked credentials or unusual activity. A risk policy can require a secure password change when risk is high.\n\n- **B** is not a risk signal.\n- **C** is a password policy matter.\n- **D** usually lowers risk.\n\n**Exam tip:** user risk equals compromised credentials; the response is often a password reset.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Identity Protection'),
Q(43, 'identity', 'Identity Protection - sign-in risk',
  'A sign-in is flagged as high risk because it comes from an anonymous IP address and an unfamiliar location. What does sign-in risk measure?',
  ['The likelihood that this specific authentication attempt is not the legitimate user', 'The likelihood that the account password has been leaked', 'The number of failed applications on the device', 'The cost of the Azure subscription'],
  ['A'],
  'Sign-in risk scores one authentication attempt using signals such as anonymous IP use, atypical travel, malware-linked IP addresses, and unfamiliar properties. A risk policy can require MFA rather than a password change.\n\n- **B** is user risk.\n- **C** is unrelated.\n- **D** is billing.\n\n**Exam tip:** user risk is about the account; sign-in risk is about this attempt.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Identity Protection', { diagram: 'identity-protection' }),
Q(44, 'identity', 'Risk policies',
  'Which response is typical for a sign-in risk policy when risk is high?',
  ['Require multi-factor authentication', 'Delete the user account', 'Remove all group memberships', 'Disable Conditional Access'],
  ['A'],
  'Risk policies respond proportionally: a high sign-in risk commonly requires MFA, while high user risk commonly requires a secure password change. Both can also block access, and both need Microsoft Entra ID P2.\n\n- **B** is destructive and not automatic.\n- **C** is not a risk response.\n- **D** would weaken protection.\n\n**Exam tip:** sign-in risk maps to MFA; user risk maps to password reset.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Identity Protection'),
Q(45, 'identity', 'Privileged Identity Management',
  'Administrators need Global Administrator rights only while performing a change. Which Entra feature should be used?',
  ['Privileged Identity Management with eligible, time-bound assignments', 'Permanent Global Administrator assignment', 'A shared administrator account', 'A Conditional Access policy that blocks administrators'],
  ['A'],
  'PIM makes a role eligible rather than permanently active. The administrator activates it for a limited period, optionally with approval, MFA, and a justification, and every activation is audited.\n\n- **B** is the always-on risk PIM removes.\n- **C** destroys accountability.\n- **D** prevents the work from being done.\n\n**Exam tip:** just-in-time elevation with approval equals PIM; it requires Entra ID P2.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Privileged Identity Management'),
Q(46, 'identity', 'PIM vs permanent roles',
  'Why is a permanent Global Administrator assignment riskier than an eligible PIM assignment?',
  ['The account holds powerful access all the time, so a compromise is immediately privileged', 'Permanent roles cannot be audited', 'Eligible roles do not require authentication', 'Permanent roles block Conditional Access'],
  ['A'],
  'Standing access is available to an attacker the moment the account is compromised. Eligibility removes standing access, forces deliberate activation, and records who activated which role and why.\n\n- **B** is false; both are audited.\n- **C** is false; activation still requires authentication and often MFA.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** reduce standing privilege first, then add approval and MFA to activation.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Privileged Identity Management'),
Q(47, 'identity', 'Entra roles vs Azure RBAC',
  'What is the difference between Microsoft Entra roles and Azure RBAC roles?',
  ['Entra roles manage directory objects; Azure RBAC manages Azure resources', 'Entra roles manage Azure resources; Azure RBAC manages directory objects', 'They are the same set of roles', 'Azure RBAC applies only to Microsoft 365 mailboxes'],
  ['A'],
  'Entra roles, such as User Administrator or Global Administrator, control the directory: users, groups, applications, and identity policies. Azure RBAC roles, such as Owner or Contributor, control Azure resources at a management group, subscription, resource group, or resource scope.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is false.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** directory equals Entra role; Azure resource equals Azure RBAC role.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe role-based access control'),
Q(48, 'identity', 'Azure RBAC scope',
  'A role assignment is made at a subscription. What happens to resources in its resource groups?',
  ['The permission is inherited by every resource group and resource in the subscription', 'Only the subscription object is affected', 'Each resource group must be assigned separately', 'The permission applies only to billing'],
  ['A'],
  'Azure RBAC assignments inherit downward. Assigning at a management group covers all subscriptions beneath it, assigning at a subscription covers its resource groups, and assigning at a resource group covers its resources.\n\n- **B** ignores inheritance.\n- **C** would duplicate work.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** assign at the highest scope that still respects least privilege.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe role-based access control'),
Q(49, 'identity', 'Administrative units',
  'A university wants department administrators to manage only users in their own department. Which Entra feature should be used?',
  ['Administrative units', 'A management group', 'An access package', 'A Conditional Access policy'],
  ['A'],
  'An administrative unit is a container of users, groups, or devices. A scoped administrator role assigned on the unit lets a department administrator manage those objects without rights over the rest of the tenant.\n\n- **B** groups Azure subscriptions, not directory objects.\n- **C** packages access for users.\n- **D** controls sign-in conditions.\n\n**Exam tip:** delegated administration over a subset of objects equals administrative units.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe role-based access control'),
Q(50, 'identity', 'Access reviews',
  'A manager must confirm every quarter that team members still need access to a finance app. Which Entra governance feature fits?',
  ['Access reviews', 'Conditional Access', 'Password protection', 'Self-service password reset'],
  ['A'],
  'Access reviews ask reviewers - managers, group owners, or the users themselves - to confirm or remove access on a schedule. They produce an audit trail and can automatically remove access that is not reconfirmed.\n\n- **B** evaluates sign-in conditions.\n- **C** blocks weak passwords.\n- **D** recovers accounts.\n\n**Exam tip:** recurring recertification equals access reviews, part of Entra ID Governance.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe access reviews'),
Q(51, 'identity', 'Entitlement management',
  'A new starter needs a standard bundle of app access, group membership, and SharePoint permissions. Which feature packages these together?',
  ['Entitlement management with access packages', 'A dynamic security group', 'An administrative unit', 'Privileged Identity Management'],
  ['A'],
  'Entitlement management defines access packages that bundle resources with a policy: who may request, whether approval is needed, how long access lasts, and when it is reviewed. Requests can be self-service.\n\n- **B** automates membership by attribute but does not bundle or govern multiple resources.\n- **C** scopes administration.\n- **D** elevates roles.\n\n**Exam tip:** a bundle of resources with request and expiry policies equals an access package.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe entitlement management'),
Q(52, 'identity', 'Lifecycle workflows',
  'Which Entra capability automates onboarding tasks such as sending a welcome email and adding a new hire to groups?',
  ['Lifecycle workflows', 'Access reviews', 'Conditional Access', 'Microsoft Entra Connect'],
  ['A'],
  'Lifecycle workflows trigger on events such as a new hire, a mover, or a leaver, and run tasks automatically: assign groups, send emails, disable accounts, and remove access. They reduce manual joiner-mover-leaver work.\n\n- **B** recertifies access periodically.\n- **C** controls sign-ins.\n- **D** synchronises identities.\n\n**Exam tip:** event-driven joiner and leaver automation equals lifecycle workflows.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe lifecycle workflows'),
Q(53, 'identity', 'Single sign-on',
  'What does single sign-on provide?',
  ['One authentication gives a user access to multiple applications', 'One password is shared by all users', 'Every application bypasses MFA', 'Users no longer need individual accounts'],
  ['A'],
  'SSO centralises authentication in the identity provider. The user signs in once and receives tokens for each application, which reduces password fatigue and the number of credentials an attacker can steal.\n\n- **B** is insecure and unrelated.\n- **C** is false; policies can still require MFA per app.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** SSO is one identity and one sign-in, not one shared password.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe single sign-on'),
Q(54, 'identity', 'Federation',
  'What is federation in identity?',
  ['A trust relationship that lets another identity provider authenticate users for your applications', 'Storing all passwords in one database', 'Joining two tenants into one directory', 'Synchronising files between regions'],
  ['A'],
  'Federation establishes trust between an identity provider and a relying party, so assertions from the provider are accepted. An organisation can federate its on-premises Active Directory Federation Services with Entra ID instead of synchronising password hashes.\n\n- **B** is credential storage, not federation.\n- **C** is not what federation does.\n- **D** is file replication.\n\n**Exam tip:** federation is about trusting assertions from another provider.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe single sign-on'),
Q(55, 'identity', 'SAML',
  'Which protocol uses XML assertions to exchange authentication and authorisation data between an identity provider and an application?',
  ['SAML', 'OAuth 2.0', 'Kerberos', 'LDAP'],
  ['A'],
  'Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) is an XML-based standard for SSO. The identity provider issues a signed assertion about the user, and the application trusts it to grant access.\n\n- **B** delegates authorisation with access tokens and is not XML-based.\n- **C** is an on-premises ticket protocol.\n- **D** is a directory access protocol.\n\n**Exam tip:** SAML equals XML assertions for enterprise SSO.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe single sign-on'),
Q(56, 'identity', 'OpenID Connect',
  'Which protocol adds an identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0 and issues an ID token?',
  ['OpenID Connect', 'SAML', 'Kerberos', 'RADIUS'],
  ['A'],
  'OpenID Connect (OIDC) uses OAuth 2.0 flows for authorisation and adds authentication: the provider returns an ID token containing claims about the signed-in user, which modern web and mobile apps use for SSO.\n\n- **B** is XML-based and older for web SSO.\n- **C** is a domain ticket protocol.\n- **D** is network access authentication.\n\n**Exam tip:** OIDC equals identity on top of OAuth 2.0.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe single sign-on'),
Q(57, 'identity', 'OAuth 2.0',
  'What is OAuth 2.0 designed to do?',
  ['Delegate authorisation so an app can access resources without the user password', 'Authenticate a user and prove identity directly', 'Encrypt files at rest', 'Synchronise directories between tenants'],
  ['A'],
  'OAuth 2.0 is an authorisation framework: the user grants a client limited access, and the client receives an access token for a resource. It does not itself authenticate the user, which is why OpenID Connect is layered on top.\n\n- **B** is OIDC, not OAuth alone.\n- **C** is encryption.\n- **D** is directory synchronisation.\n\n**Exam tip:** OAuth is about delegated access; OIDC is about identity.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe single sign-on'),
Q(58, 'identity', 'Managed identities',
  'An Azure app must read from a storage account without storing a secret. What should it use?',
  ['A managed identity', 'A shared administrator password', 'A client secret in source code', 'A guest user account'],
  ['A'],
  'A managed identity is an identity created and managed by Azure for a resource such as an app service or virtual machine. The platform handles the credential, so there is no secret to store, rotate, or leak, and Azure RBAC grants the required access.\n\n- **B** violates least privilege.\n- **C** is exactly the risk being avoided.\n- **D** is for people, not workloads.\n\n**Exam tip:** service-to-service without secrets equals a managed identity.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe managed identities'),
Q(59, 'identity', 'App registration and service principal',
  'What is created in a tenant when an application is registered with Microsoft Entra ID?',
  ['An application object and a service principal that represents it in the tenant', 'A new tenant and subscription', 'A guest user for each customer', 'A permanent Global Administrator account'],
  ['A'],
  'The application object holds the app definition, while the service principal is the local instance that defines what the app can do in this tenant. Permissions are granted to the service principal.\n\n- **B** is unrelated.\n- **C** is not created automatically.\n- **D** would be a severe security flaw.\n\n**Exam tip:** app object equals the definition; service principal equals the local identity.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe application identities'),
Q(60, 'identity', 'Hybrid identity',
  'A company wants on-premises Active Directory users to sign in to Microsoft 365 with the same account. Which tool synchronises the directories?',
  ['Microsoft Entra Connect Sync', 'Azure Migrate', 'Microsoft Defender for Identity', 'Azure Bastion'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft Entra Connect synchronises users, groups, and attributes from on-premises Active Directory to Entra ID, and can also enable password hash sync, pass-through authentication, or federation.\n\n- **B** migrates servers and workloads.\n- **C** monitors identity-based attacks.\n- **D** provides management access to virtual machines.\n\n**Exam tip:** one identity for on-premises and cloud equals hybrid identity with Entra Connect.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe hybrid identity'),
Q(61, 'identity', 'Password hash synchronisation',
  'How does password hash synchronisation support hybrid sign-in?',
  ['A hash of the on-premises password hash is synchronised so Entra ID can validate the password', 'The plain-text password is copied to the cloud', 'The user must maintain two separate passwords', 'Entra ID sends every sign-in to a federation server'],
  ['A'],
  'Entra Connect takes the on-premises password hash, hashes it again, and synchronises that value. Entra ID validates cloud sign-ins against it, so users keep one password and the on-premises password itself never leaves the network.\n\n- **B** would be a breach.\n- **C** describes separate credentials.\n- **D** describes federation, not hash sync.\n\n**Exam tip:** PHS is the simplest hybrid option and enables leaked-credential detection.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe hybrid identity'),
Q(62, 'identity', 'Pass-through authentication',
  'Which hybrid option validates passwords directly against on-premises Active Directory without storing password hashes in the cloud?',
  ['Pass-through authentication', 'Password hash synchronisation', 'Seamless single sign-on', 'Federation with AD FS'],
  ['A'],
  'Pass-through authentication installs lightweight agents that validate sign-ins against on-premises Active Directory in real time. No password hash is stored in Entra ID, but availability depends on the agents and connectivity.\n\n- **B** stores a hash in the cloud.\n- **C** reduces prompts on domain-joined devices but does not validate the password itself.\n- **D** uses a federation server and claims.\n\n**Exam tip:** PTA validates on-premises; PHS validates in the cloud.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe hybrid identity'),
Q(63, 'identity', 'Seamless single sign-on',
  'What does Microsoft Entra seamless single sign-on provide for domain-joined devices on the corporate network?',
  ['Users are signed in automatically without re-entering credentials', 'Users bypass all Conditional Access policies', 'Administrators can reset passwords without audit', 'Guests receive permanent access'],
  ['A'],
  'Seamless SSO uses Kerberos tickets from the on-premises domain to sign users in to Entra ID automatically when they are on a domain-joined device with network line of sight to a domain controller.\n\n- **B** is false; policies still apply.\n- **C** would violate accountability.\n- **D** is unrelated.\n\n**Exam tip:** seamless SSO removes prompts; it does not remove policy.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe hybrid identity'),
Q(64, 'identity', 'Temporary Access Pass',
  'A new employee has no security key or phone yet. Which feature lets an administrator issue a time-limited passcode for first-time sign-in?',
  ['Temporary Access Pass', 'A permanent password', 'A shared guest account', 'A service principal secret'],
  ['A'],
  'A Temporary Access Pass is a time-limited code that lets a user register passwordless methods or recover access. It can be single-use, is bound by policy, and is audited.\n\n- **B** creates a standing credential.\n- **C** destroys accountability.\n- **D** is for applications, not people.\n\n**Exam tip:** onboarding and recovery without a phone equals Temporary Access Pass.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods'),
Q(65, 'identity', 'Cross-tenant access settings',
  'A bank wants to define how guests from a partner tenant authenticate and whether their MFA claims are trusted. What should it configure?',
  ['Cross-tenant access settings', 'An administrative unit', 'A dynamic group', 'A password protection policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Cross-tenant access settings control inbound and outbound B2B collaboration and direct connect, including whether to trust multi-factor and device claims from the partner tenant and which users may be invited.\n\n- **B** scopes administration.\n- **C** builds membership by attribute.\n- **D** blocks weak passwords.\n\n**Exam tip:** partner-tenant trust and guest policy equal cross-tenant access settings.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe external identities'),
Q(66, 'identity', 'Identity Protection vs Conditional Access',
  'How do Identity Protection and Conditional Access work together?',
  ['Identity Protection detects risk; Conditional Access enforces the response to that risk', 'Conditional Access detects risk; Identity Protection enforces policy', 'Both only report on sign-in volume', 'They are the same feature with two names'],
  ['A'],
  'Identity Protection calculates user and sign-in risk from signals. Conditional Access can use that risk as a condition and require MFA, a password change, or block access. Risk policies in Identity Protection provide a simpler automatic response.\n\n- **B** reverses the roles.\n- **C** is false.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** risk detection is Identity Protection; the policy decision is Conditional Access.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe Identity Protection'),
Q(67, 'identity', 'Phishing-resistant authentication',
  'Which two authentication methods are considered phishing-resistant? (Select two.)',
  ['FIDO2 security keys', 'Windows Hello for Business', 'SMS codes', 'Security questions'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Phishing-resistant methods bind the credential to the site origin or device, so a fake sign-in page cannot capture and replay it. FIDO2 keys and Windows Hello for Business both use cryptographic challenges bound to the relying party.\n\n- **C** can be intercepted or relayed by an attacker.\n- **D** is knowledge-based and easily guessed or researched.\n\n**Exam tip:** phishing-resistant equals FIDO2 or Windows Hello for Business.',
  'Describe identity and access management concepts - Describe authentication methods')
,
Q(68, 'solutions', 'Defender XDR - correlation',
  'A security operations centre wants alerts from email, endpoint, identity, and cloud apps combined into a single incident with one investigation timeline. Which Microsoft solution provides this?',
  ['Microsoft Defender XDR', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud', 'Microsoft Purview', 'Microsoft Entra ID Governance'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft Defender XDR is the extended detection and response layer above the Defender workload products. It correlates signals from Defender for Office 365, Endpoint, Identity, and Cloud Apps into incidents, so analysts investigate one story rather than four separate alert queues.\n\n- **B** assesses and protects cloud infrastructure posture.\n- **C** is the compliance and data-governance suite.\n- **D** governs identity lifecycle and access.\n\n**Exam tip:** XDR correlates across Microsoft security products; Sentinel correlates across the whole estate, including third-party sources.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender XDR', { diagram: 'security-stack' }),
Q(69, 'solutions', 'Defender XDR - incidents',
  'What is an incident in Microsoft Defender XDR?',
  ['A collection of related alerts that together describe one attack', 'A single antivirus signature update', 'A list of all users in the tenant', 'A billing record for Azure consumption'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender XDR groups related alerts - for example a phishing email, a suspicious sign-in, and a PowerShell launch - into one incident. Analysts triage the incident as a whole, which preserves context and reduces duplicate work.\n\n- **B** is a definition update.\n- **C** is directory data.\n- **D** is billing.\n\n**Exam tip:** alert equals one detection; incident equals the related story.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender XDR'),
Q(70, 'solutions', 'Defender XDR - automated investigation',
  'Which Defender XDR capability automatically investigates alerts, gathers evidence, and can remove a threat without analyst action?',
  ['Automated investigation and response', 'Advanced hunting', 'Secure Score', 'Data connectors'],
  ['A'],
  'Automated investigation and response (AIR) runs playbooks against alerts, collects evidence such as files and processes, decides whether the threat is malicious, and can remediate - quarantine a file, isolate a device, or disable an account - subject to the configured approval settings.\n\n- **B** is a manual query interface.\n- **C** measures posture.\n- **D** ingests data into Sentinel, not a response engine.\n\n**Exam tip:** automated investigation and response reduces mean time to respond; advanced hunting is the manual counterpart.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender XDR'),
Q(71, 'solutions', 'Defender XDR - advanced hunting',
  'An analyst needs to write a custom query across up to 30 days of raw endpoint, email, and identity events. Which Defender XDR feature should be used?',
  ['Advanced hunting with KQL', 'The regulatory compliance dashboard', 'Secure Score improvement actions', 'Attack surface reduction rules'],
  ['A'],
  'Advanced hunting is a query-based threat-hunting tool that uses Kusto Query Language (KQL) over raw telemetry retained for up to 30 days. Analysts can hunt proactively for behaviours that no existing detection covers and turn a query into a custom detection.\n\n- **B** tracks compliance controls.\n- **C** lists hardening tasks.\n- **D** blocks behaviours in real time rather than querying history.\n\n**Exam tip:** KQL plus a 30-day raw-event window equals advanced hunting.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender XDR'),
Q(72, 'solutions', 'Defender for Endpoint - purpose',
  'Which Microsoft product protects Windows, macOS, Linux, and mobile devices from malware and ransomware?',
  ['Microsoft Defender for Endpoint', 'Microsoft Defender for Identity', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Sentinel'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Endpoint is the endpoint protection platform: antivirus, attack surface reduction, device control, endpoint detection and response, and vulnerability management for laptops, servers, and mobile devices.\n\n- **B** monitors on-premises Active Directory, not devices.\n- **C** governs SaaS applications.\n- **D** is a SIEM.\n\n**Exam tip:** if the asset is a device, the answer is Defender for Endpoint.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Endpoint'),
Q(73, 'solutions', 'Defender for Endpoint - attack surface reduction',
  'Which capability blocks risky behaviours such as Office applications launching child processes or scripts downloading executable content?',
  ['Attack surface reduction rules', 'Safe Attachments', 'Access reviews', 'Retention labels'],
  ['A'],
  'Attack surface reduction (ASR) rules target behaviours malware commonly needs, such as Office creating child processes, script files executing downloaded content, or credential theft from lsass. They reduce the attack surface without relying on signature detection.\n\n- **B** detonates email attachments.\n- **C** recertifies access.\n- **D** retains content.\n\n**Exam tip:** ASR blocks behaviour; antivirus blocks known malicious files.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Endpoint'),
Q(74, 'solutions', 'Defender for Endpoint - vulnerability management',
  'A team needs a prioritised list of missing patches, exposed software, and misconfigurations across its device estate. Which capability provides this?',
  ['Threat and vulnerability management', 'Safe Links', 'Cloud Discovery', 'Communication compliance'],
  ['A'],
  'Threat and vulnerability management (also called vulnerability management) inventories software, identifies missing security updates and misconfigurations, and prioritises them using threat intelligence and exposure, so remediation effort goes to the risks that matter.\n\n- **B** checks URLs at click time.\n- **C** discovers SaaS use.\n- **D** reviews messages for policy breaches.\n\n**Exam tip:** vulnerability management finds the weakness; ASR and patching close it.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Endpoint'),
Q(75, 'solutions', 'Defender for Endpoint - EDR',
  'Which capability records device behaviour and lets an analyst trace how an attack unfolded over time?',
  ['Endpoint detection and response', 'Anti-spam filtering', 'Records management', 'Entitlement management'],
  ['A'],
  'Endpoint detection and response (EDR) continuously records process, file, network, and registry activity, detects suspicious behaviour, and provides an investigation timeline with response actions such as isolating a device or collecting an investigation package.\n\n- **B** filters email.\n- **C** governs retention.\n- **D** packages access for users.\n\n**Exam tip:** EDR is behaviour-based detection and investigation, not just signature scanning.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Endpoint'),
Q(76, 'solutions', 'Defender for Endpoint - plans',
  'A company requires endpoint detection and response, vulnerability management, and automated investigation. Which plan meets the requirement?',
  ['Defender for Endpoint Plan 2', 'Defender for Endpoint Plan 1', 'Defender for Office 365 Plan 1', 'Defender for Cloud Apps'],
  ['A'],
  'Plan 1 provides core endpoint protection: antimalware, attack surface reduction, device control, web content filtering, and application control. Plan 2 adds EDR, threat and vulnerability management, automated investigation and response, advanced hunting, and Microsoft Threat Experts.\n\n- **B** lacks EDR and vulnerability management.\n- **C** protects email and collaboration, not devices.\n- **D** governs SaaS apps.\n\n**Exam tip:** EDR, TVM, and advanced hunting are the Plan 2 markers.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Endpoint'),
Q(77, 'solutions', 'Defender for Endpoint - capabilities',
  'Which two capabilities are provided by Microsoft Defender for Endpoint? (Select two.)',
  ['Endpoint detection and response', 'Threat and vulnerability management', 'Safe Attachments detonation for email', 'Discovery of unsanctioned SaaS applications'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defender for Endpoint covers device protection end to end: EDR for behaviour-based detection and investigation, and vulnerability management for prioritised patching and configuration fixes.\n\n- **C** belongs to Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** belongs to Defender for Cloud Apps.\n\n**Exam tip:** map the asset first - device to Endpoint, email to Office 365, SaaS to Cloud Apps.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Endpoint'),
Q(78, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 - Safe Links',
  'Users click URLs in email and Teams. Which feature checks the destination at the time of the click and blocks malicious pages?',
  ['Safe Links', 'Safe Attachments', 'Anti-spam policy', 'Attack surface reduction'],
  ['A'],
  'Safe Links rewrites and inspects URLs when the user clicks, so a link that was benign at delivery but malicious later is still blocked. It also scans links in Teams conversations and Office documents.\n\n- **B** detonates attachments, not links.\n- **C** filters bulk and junk mail.\n- **D** blocks device behaviours.\n\n**Exam tip:** click-time URL protection equals Safe Links.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Office 365'),
Q(79, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 - Safe Attachments',
  'An organisation wants every email attachment opened in a sandbox before it reaches the recipient. Which feature provides this?',
  ['Safe Attachments', 'Safe Links', 'Anti-phishing impersonation protection', 'Communication compliance'],
  ['A'],
  'Safe Attachments detonates attachments in a sandbox, observing behaviour to detect malicious content that signature scanning would miss. Policy can block, replace, or deliver after scanning (dynamic delivery).\n\n- **B** protects URLs.\n- **C** detects impersonation in the message itself.\n- **D** reviews message content for policy violations.\n\n**Exam tip:** attachment detonation equals Safe Attachments.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Office 365'),
Q(80, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 - anti-phishing',
  'An attacker registers a lookalike domain and emails staff pretending to be the finance director. Which capability specifically detects this?',
  ['Anti-phishing policies with impersonation protection', 'Safe Links', 'Retention policies', 'Access packages'],
  ['A'],
  'Anti-phishing policies use mailbox intelligence, spoof intelligence, and impersonation protection for users, domains, and trusted senders. They detect lookalike domains and display-name tricks that ordinary spam filters miss.\n\n- **B** inspects URLs after delivery.\n- **C** controls retention.\n- **D** bundles access.\n\n**Exam tip:** impersonation and spoofing are anti-phishing, not anti-spam.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Office 365'),
Q(81, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 - anti-spam',
  'Which policy controls bulk mail, junk email, and quarantined messages for Exchange Online?',
  ['Anti-spam policy', 'Safe Attachments policy', 'Insider risk policy', 'Conditional Access policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Anti-spam policies score inbound messages and act on spam, high-confidence spam, bulk mail, and phishing, with quarantine and allow/block lists. They are a separate policy type from anti-phishing and Safe Attachments.\n\n- **B** handles attachments.\n- **C** detects insider risk.\n- **D** controls sign-ins.\n\n**Exam tip:** spam volume equals anti-spam; targeted impersonation equals anti-phishing.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Office 365'),
Q(82, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 - plans',
  'A company wants automated investigation and response, Threat Explorer, and attack simulation training for email. Which plan is required?',
  ['Defender for Office 365 Plan 2', 'Defender for Office 365 Plan 1', 'Defender for Endpoint Plan 2', 'Exchange Online Protection'],
  ['A'],
  'Plan 1 provides Safe Attachments, Safe Links, and anti-phishing. Plan 2 adds automated investigation and response, Threat Explorer and Threat Trackers, campaign views, and attack simulation training.\n\n- **B** lacks those investigation and simulation features.\n- **C** is a device product.\n- **D** is the baseline protection included with Exchange Online.\n\n**Exam tip:** Threat Explorer and attack simulation training are Plan 2 markers.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Office 365'),
Q(83, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 - attack simulation',
  'A security team wants to send realistic phishing messages to staff and assign remedial training to anyone who fails. Which feature should be used?',
  ['Attack simulation training', 'Safe Links', 'Advanced hunting', 'Access reviews'],
  ['A'],
  'Attack simulation training runs controlled phishing simulations, records who clicked or entered credentials, and automatically assigns training. It measures human risk and improves awareness without exposing the organisation to real attackers.\n\n- **B** blocks live URLs.\n- **C** queries telemetry.\n- **D** recertifies access.\n\n**Exam tip:** simulated phishing plus training equals attack simulation training.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Office 365'),
Q(84, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 - threats',
  'Which two threats does Microsoft Defender for Office 365 primarily address? (Select two.)',
  ['Phishing messages that impersonate a trusted sender', 'Email attachments that carry malware', 'Unpatched vulnerabilities on domain controllers', 'Shadow IT SaaS applications'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defender for Office 365 protects the email and collaboration channel: anti-phishing, anti-spam, Safe Links, and Safe Attachments all operate on messages, attachments, and links.\n\n- **C** is Defender for Identity and vulnerability management territory.\n- **D** is Defender for Cloud Apps.\n\n**Exam tip:** email-borne threats point to Defender for Office 365.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Office 365'),
Q(85, 'solutions', 'Defender for Identity - purpose',
  'Which Microsoft product monitors on-premises Active Directory signals to detect identity-based attacks?',
  ['Microsoft Defender for Identity', 'Microsoft Defender for Endpoint', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Purview Audit'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Identity uses sensors on domain controllers and other identity infrastructure to analyse authentication traffic and detect attacks such as reconnaissance, compromised credentials, pass-the-hash, and lateral movement.\n\n- **B** protects devices.\n- **C** governs SaaS.\n- **D** records activity for investigation.\n\n**Exam tip:** on-premises Active Directory attack detection equals Defender for Identity.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Identity'),
Q(86, 'solutions', 'Defender for Identity - lateral movement',
  'An attacker uses stolen credentials to move from a workstation to several servers. Which Defender for Identity detection addresses this?',
  ['Lateral movement detection', 'Safe Attachments', 'Cloud Discovery', 'Retention hold'],
  ['A'],
  'Lateral movement paths show how an attacker hops between devices using compromised accounts. Defender for Identity identifies these paths so administrators can remove the exposed credentials and break the chain before critical systems are reached.\n\n- **B** is email protection.\n- **C** discovers SaaS use.\n- **D** preserves content.\n\n**Exam tip:** lateral movement equals identity hops between machines, not malware spreading.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Identity'),
Q(87, 'solutions', 'Defender for Identity - sensors',
  'Where are Defender for Identity sensors typically deployed?',
  ['On domain controllers and other identity infrastructure', 'On every user laptop', 'In the email transport pipeline', 'Inside each SaaS application'],
  ['A'],
  'Sensors are installed on domain controllers, AD FS, and AD CS servers, where they observe authentication and directory traffic. That placement gives visibility of identity attacks without an agent on every endpoint.\n\n- **B** is Defender for Endpoint territory.\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Defender for Cloud Apps.\n\n**Exam tip:** identity sensors sit on domain controllers; endpoint agents sit on devices.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Identity'),
Q(88, 'solutions', 'Defender for Identity vs Endpoint',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes Defender for Identity from Defender for Endpoint?',
  ['Defender for Identity monitors directory authentication; Defender for Endpoint protects devices', 'Defender for Identity protects devices; Defender for Endpoint monitors directories', 'Both protect only email', 'Both are SIEM products'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Identity watches Active Directory authentication and directory services for identity attacks. Defender for Endpoint runs on devices to prevent, detect, and investigate malware and behaviour. They feed the same Defender XDR incident stream but cover different assets.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Sentinel.\n\n**Exam tip:** directory versus device is the dividing line.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Identity'),
Q(89, 'solutions', 'Defender for Identity - detections',
  'Which two attack behaviours does Defender for Identity detect? (Select two.)',
  ['Account reconnaissance and directory enumeration', 'Compromised credentials used in pass-the-hash', 'Malicious macros in a Word attachment', 'Unmanaged SaaS applications'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defender for Identity focuses on the identity attack chain: reconnaissance and enumeration of accounts and groups, credential theft and reuse, and the lateral movement that follows.\n\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Defender for Cloud Apps.\n\n**Exam tip:** if the evidence lives in directory authentication, choose Defender for Identity.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Identity'),
Q(90, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud Apps - shadow IT',
  'A company wants to discover which unsanctioned SaaS applications employees use. Which capability should be used?',
  ['Cloud Discovery in Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Safe Links', 'Privileged Identity Management', 'Compliance Manager'],
  ['A'],
  'Cloud Discovery analyses traffic logs from firewalls and proxies to build an inventory of cloud apps in use, with risk scores for each app. Security teams can then sanction, unsanction, or block apps by policy.\n\n- **B** protects URLs.\n- **C** elevates roles.\n- **D** tracks regulatory assessments.\n\n**Exam tip:** unknown SaaS equals shadow IT, and shadow IT equals Cloud Discovery.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps'),
Q(91, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud Apps - session controls',
  'A bank wants to block downloads from an unmanaged device while still allowing users to read a sanctioned SaaS app. Which capability provides this?',
  ['Conditional Access App Control with session policies', 'Safe Attachments', 'Retention labels', 'Access reviews'],
  ['A'],
  'Conditional Access App Control uses a reverse proxy so sessions to a SaaS app pass through Defender for Cloud Apps. Session policies can block download, copy, or print, and apply sensitivity labels, without changing the app itself.\n\n- **B** handles email attachments.\n- **C** governs retention.\n- **D** recertifies access.\n\n**Exam tip:** real-time control inside a SaaS session equals Conditional Access App Control.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps'),
Q(92, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud Apps - app connectors',
  'Which Defender for Cloud Apps feature uses application programming interfaces to read activity and files directly from a sanctioned SaaS app?',
  ['App connectors', 'Cloud Discovery', 'Safe Links', 'Attack surface reduction'],
  ['A'],
  'App connectors authorise API access to a SaaS provider, giving visibility of activity, files, and permissions, plus the ability to apply governance actions such as quarantine or remove sharing. Cloud Discovery, by contrast, infers usage from network traffic.\n\n- **B** is traffic-log based.\n- **C** is URL protection.\n- **D** is endpoint hardening.\n\n**Exam tip:** API-level visibility equals an app connector; traffic-level visibility equals Cloud Discovery.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps'),
Q(93, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud Apps vs Defender for Cloud',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes Defender for Cloud Apps from Defender for Cloud?',
  ['Cloud Apps governs SaaS use; Defender for Cloud protects cloud infrastructure workloads', 'Cloud Apps protects Azure virtual machines; Defender for Cloud governs SaaS', 'Both only protect email', 'Both are identity providers'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Cloud Apps is a cloud access security broker for SaaS: discovery, app governance, session control, and anomaly detection. Defender for Cloud is a cloud-native application protection platform for infrastructure: posture management, recommendations, and workload protection across Azure, AWS, and GCP.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Entra ID.\n\n**Exam tip:** SaaS equals Cloud Apps; servers and databases equals Defender for Cloud.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps'),
Q(94, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud Apps - anomalies',
  'Defender for Cloud Apps raises an alert because one account signed in from two distant countries within an hour. Which capability produced this alert?',
  ['Anomaly detection policies', 'Safe Attachments', 'Retention labels', 'Secure Score'],
  ['A'],
  'Anomaly detection policies learn normal behaviour per user and app, then flag deviations such as impossible travel, unusual file download volume, or activity from a new location. They require an app connector or proxy to supply the activity data.\n\n- **B** is email detonation.\n- **C** is retention.\n- **D** is posture scoring.\n\n**Exam tip:** impossible travel in a SaaS app equals a Cloud Apps anomaly detection policy.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps'),
Q(95, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud Apps - capabilities',
  'Which two capabilities belong to Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps? (Select two.)',
  ['Discovering shadow IT from network traffic logs', 'Controlling downloads in a SaaS session', 'Detonating email attachments', 'Managing Azure subscription permissions'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defender for Cloud Apps is a cloud access security broker: it discovers SaaS use, assesses app risk, governs sanctioned apps through APIs, and controls sessions in real time through Conditional Access App Control.\n\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Azure RBAC.\n\n**Exam tip:** visibility and control of SaaS equals Cloud Apps.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps'),
Q(96, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud - CSPM',
  'Which Microsoft capability continuously assesses Azure, AWS, and GCP resources against security best practices and lists misconfigurations?',
  ['Cloud security posture management in Microsoft Defender for Cloud', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Sentinel', 'Microsoft Purview Data Map'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Cloud includes cloud security posture management (CSPM). It evaluates resources against benchmarks such as the Microsoft cloud security benchmark and CIS, surfaces misconfigurations as recommendations, and calculates a secure score.\n\n- **B** governs SaaS apps.\n- **C** is a SIEM.\n- **D** catalogues data assets.\n\n**Exam tip:** misconfiguration assessment equals CSPM in Defender for Cloud.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud'),
Q(97, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud - recommendations',
  'Defender for Cloud reports that a storage account allows public access. What is this finding called?',
  ['A security recommendation', 'A playbook', 'A sensitivity label', 'An access review'],
  ['A'],
  'Recommendations are actionable hardening tasks produced when a resource fails a control. Each recommendation explains the risk, shows affected resources, and offers quick fix or remediation steps.\n\n- **B** is a Sentinel automation.\n- **C** classifies content.\n- **D** recertifies access.\n\n**Exam tip:** a failed control becomes a recommendation; fixing it raises the secure score.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud'),
Q(98, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud - regulatory compliance',
  'A compliance officer wants to see how Azure and AWS resources map to ISO 27001 and PCI DSS controls inside Defender for Cloud. Which feature provides this?',
  ['The regulatory compliance dashboard', 'Safe Links policies', 'Communication compliance', 'Access packages'],
  ['A'],
  'The regulatory compliance dashboard in Defender for Cloud shows assessments against standards such as ISO 27001, PCI DSS, SOC 2, and NIST, with passing and failing controls and the recommendations that remediate each failure.\n\n- **B** is email URL protection.\n- **C** reviews communications.\n- **D** bundles access.\n\n**Exam tip:** technical control mapping to a standard equals the Defender for Cloud regulatory compliance dashboard.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud'),
Q(99, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud - workload protection',
  'Which Defender for Cloud capability protects servers, containers, storage accounts, and databases from threats?',
  ['Defender workload protection plans', 'Cloud Discovery', 'Privileged Identity Management', 'Retention policies'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Cloud offers per-workload plans - Defender for Servers, Containers, Storage, SQL databases, App Service, Key Vault, and more - that add threat detection and advanced protection beyond the free posture assessment.\n\n- **B** is a Cloud Apps feature.\n- **C** is Entra governance.\n- **D** is Purview retention.\n\n**Exam tip:** posture is free and foundational; workload protection is the paid plan layer.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud'),
Q(100, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud - multicloud',
  'A company runs workloads in Azure and AWS and wants one security posture view. Which statement is correct?',
  ['Defender for Cloud can connect AWS and GCP accounts and assess them alongside Azure', 'Defender for Cloud supports Azure only', 'AWS requires Microsoft Sentinel instead', 'Multicloud support needs Defender for Cloud Apps'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Cloud natively extends to AWS and GCP through connectors, giving one secure score, one recommendation list, and workload protection across clouds. This is why it is described as a multicloud protection platform.\n\n- **B** is false.\n- **C** is false; Sentinel is a SIEM, not a prerequisite.\n- **D** is the SaaS security product.\n\n**Exam tip:** Defender for Cloud is multicloud by design.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud'),
Q(101, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud - just-in-time access',
  'A team wants management ports on virtual machines closed by default and opened only on request for a limited time. Which capability provides this?',
  ['Just-in-time VM access in Defender for Servers', 'Safe Attachments', 'Entitlement management', 'Cloud Discovery'],
  ['A'],
  'Just-in-time (JIT) VM access blocks inbound management ports such as RDP and SSH by default and opens them for a requested window from an approved source. This shrinks exposure to brute-force and scanning attacks.\n\n- **B** is email protection.\n- **C** packages user access.\n- **D** discovers SaaS.\n\n**Exam tip:** closing management ports until needed equals JIT VM access.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud'),
Q(102, 'solutions', 'Defender for Cloud - capabilities',
  'Which two capabilities does Microsoft Defender for Cloud provide? (Select two.)',
  ['Continuous security posture assessment against benchmarks', 'Threat protection for cloud workloads', 'Email attachment detonation', 'SaaS session download blocking'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defender for Cloud combines CSPM - continuous assessment, recommendations, secure score, and regulatory compliance - with CWPP, the workload protection plans that detect threats against servers, storage, databases, and containers.\n\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Defender for Cloud Apps.\n\n**Exam tip:** posture plus workload protection equals Defender for Cloud.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud'),
Q(103, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - SIEM',
  'A security team needs a cloud-native SIEM that collects logs from Microsoft 365, Azure, on-premises servers, and third-party firewalls. Which product should be deployed?',
  ['Microsoft Sentinel', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Purview'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft Sentinel is the cloud-native security information and event management (SIEM) service. It ingests logs from many sources through data connectors, applies analytics rules to detect threats, and raises incidents for investigation.\n\n- **B** assesses and protects cloud workloads.\n- **C** governs SaaS applications.\n- **D** is the compliance suite.\n\n**Exam tip:** SIEM equals Sentinel. If the requirement mentions third-party or multi-source log collection, Sentinel is the answer.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel', { diagram: 'security-stack' }),
Q(104, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - SOAR',
  'A team wants an alert to automatically open a ticket, post to Teams, and ask an analyst to approve isolating a device. Which Sentinel capability provides this?',
  ['Automation playbooks built on Azure Logic Apps', 'Analytics rules', 'Workbooks', 'Data connectors'],
  ['A'],
  'Security orchestration, automation, and response (SOAR) in Sentinel is delivered through playbooks - Logic Apps workflows triggered by an incident or alert - that can call other services, wait for approval, and run response actions.\n\n- **B** detect threats and create incidents.\n- **C** visualise data.\n- **D** ingest data.\n\n**Exam tip:** detection is an analytics rule; automated action is a playbook.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(105, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - analytics rules',
  'Which Sentinel component contains the logic that detects suspicious activity and creates an alert or incident?',
  ['An analytics rule', 'A data connector', 'A workbook', 'A retention label'],
  ['A'],
  'Analytics rules query the ingested data on a schedule or in near real time, compare it with a condition or anomaly model, and produce alerts that Sentinel groups into incidents. Scheduled, Microsoft Security, fusion, ML behaviour analytics, and anomaly rules are the main types.\n\n- **B** brings data in.\n- **C** displays it.\n- **D** retains content in Purview.\n\n**Exam tip:** detection logic lives in analytics rules.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(106, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - data connectors',
  'How does Microsoft Sentinel receive logs from Microsoft 365, Azure activity, and third-party products?',
  ['Through data connectors', 'Through retention labels', 'Through access packages', 'Through attack surface reduction rules'],
  ['A'],
  'Data connectors are the ingestion mechanism. Some connect natively to Microsoft services, others use the Common Event Format, Syslog, or the Logs Ingestion API, and many third-party products have ready-made connectors in the content hub.\n\n- **B** are Purview compliance objects.\n- **C** are Entra governance objects.\n- **D** are endpoint controls.\n\n**Exam tip:** no connector, no data - ingestion always starts with a data connector.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(107, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - workbooks',
  'Analysts need interactive dashboards that chart sign-in failures, alert volume, and incident trends over time. Which Sentinel feature should be used?',
  ['Workbooks', 'Playbooks', 'Data connectors', 'Analytics rules'],
  ['A'],
  'Workbooks are Azure Monitor workbooks adapted for Sentinel. They combine queries, charts, and tables into dashboards for monitoring operations and reporting to management.\n\n- **B** automate response.\n- **C** ingest data.\n- **D** detect threats.\n\n**Exam tip:** visualisation and reporting equals workbooks.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(108, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - workspace',
  'Where does Microsoft Sentinel store the log data that analytics rules query?',
  ['In an Azure Log Analytics workspace', 'In a SharePoint document library', 'In an Exchange Online mailbox', 'In a Microsoft Entra administrative unit'],
  ['A'],
  'Sentinel is built on a Log Analytics workspace, which stores ingested logs and provides the KQL query engine. Retention is configured on the workspace, and long-term retention can keep data for years at lower cost.\n\n- **B** stores documents.\n- **C** stores email.\n- **D** scopes directory administration.\n\n**Exam tip:** the workspace is the data store; Sentinel is the SIEM layer on top.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(109, 'solutions', 'Sentinel vs Defender XDR',
  'Which statement best describes the relationship between Microsoft Sentinel and Microsoft Defender XDR?',
  ['Defender XDR correlates Microsoft security-product signals; Sentinel is a SIEM that also ingests third-party and infrastructure logs', 'Sentinel only ingests Microsoft 365 logs; Defender XDR ingests third-party logs', 'They are the same product sold under two names', 'Defender XDR replaces Sentinel for all log collection'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender XDR is tuned for the Microsoft security products and produces high-fidelity incidents from them. Sentinel is a general SIEM and SOAR platform: it can ingest those same incidents plus Azure, on-premises, application, and third-party data, and apply custom analytics and automation across all of it. Many organisations use both, with XDR incidents flowing into Sentinel.\n\n- **B** reverses the coverage.\n- **C** is false.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** XDR for the Microsoft estate; Sentinel for the whole estate.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(110, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - capabilities',
  'Which two functions does Microsoft Sentinel provide? (Select two.)',
  ['Security information and event management', 'Security orchestration, automation, and response', 'Email attachment detonation', 'Cloud resource misconfiguration assessment'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Sentinel is a SIEM and SOAR platform: it collects and correlates logs, detects threats with analytics rules, and automates response with playbooks.\n\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Defender for Cloud posture management.\n\n**Exam tip:** SIEM plus SOAR is the Sentinel one-liner.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(111, 'solutions', 'Microsoft Secure Score',
  'What does Microsoft Secure Score measure?',
  ['The organisation security posture against recommended Microsoft 365 configurations, expressed as a percentage of achievable points', 'The number of viruses blocked in the last 24 hours', 'The uptime of Azure virtual machines', 'The number of licences assigned to users'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft Secure Score, in the Microsoft 365 Defender portal, scores the tenant against a set of improvement actions covering identity, devices, apps, data, and infrastructure. It shows points achieved against points available, plus history and comparison with similar organisations.\n\n- **B** is detection volume, not posture.\n- **C** is availability monitoring.\n- **D** is licensing.\n\n**Exam tip:** Secure Score is a posture percentage, not an incident count.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Secure Score'),
Q(112, 'solutions', 'Microsoft Secure Score - improvement actions',
  'A team wants to raise its Microsoft Secure Score. What should it do?',
  ['Complete the recommended improvement actions and re-measure', 'Delete all alerts from the portal', 'Disable multi-factor authentication to reduce friction', 'Ignore the score because it is only cosmetic'],
  ['A'],
  'Each Secure Score point comes from an improvement action: enable MFA for administrators, block legacy authentication, restrict anonymous sharing, and so on. Implementing the action and allowing the next measurement cycle to run raises the score.\n\n- **B** hides evidence rather than fixing risk.\n- **C** would lower the score and weaken security.\n- **D** is false; the score reflects real configuration.\n\n**Exam tip:** the score is a to-do list with points attached.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Secure Score'),
Q(113, 'solutions', 'PIM vs RBAC',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes Privileged Identity Management from role-based access control?',
  ['RBAC defines which permissions a role has; PIM controls when and how a user activates that role', 'PIM defines permissions; RBAC approves activation', 'Both only apply to Azure subscriptions', 'Both permanently assign administrator roles'],
  ['A'],
  'RBAC is the permission model: a role is a collection of allowed actions, and an assignment grants it to a principal. PIM adds governance on top: eligible assignment, time-bound activation, approval, MFA, and audit, so the permission is not standing.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is false; PIM also governs Entra roles and groups.\n- **D** is the opposite of PIM.\n\n**Exam tip:** RBAC says what; PIM says when.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(114, 'solutions', 'Safe Links vs Safe Attachments',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes Safe Links from Safe Attachments?',
  ['Safe Links inspects URLs at click time; Safe Attachments detonates files in a sandbox', 'Safe Links detonates files; Safe Attachments inspects URLs', 'Both only scan inbound email for spam', 'Both are features of Defender for Cloud Apps'],
  ['A'],
  'Safe Links rewrites and checks a URL when the user clicks it. Safe Attachments opens an attachment in a sandbox and observes its behaviour before delivery. Both are Defender for Office 365 features and complement each other.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** describes anti-spam.\n- **D** is the wrong product.\n\n**Exam tip:** link equals click-time; attachment equals detonation.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(115, 'solutions', 'Secure Score vs Defender for Cloud score',
  'A company compares Microsoft Secure Score with the secure score in Microsoft Defender for Cloud. Which statement is correct?',
  ['Microsoft Secure Score covers Microsoft 365 workloads; the Defender for Cloud score covers cloud resources and controls', 'Both score only Azure virtual machines', 'Microsoft Secure Score covers AWS; Defender for Cloud covers email', 'They are the same score shown in two portals'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft Secure Score measures the configuration posture of Microsoft 365 services - identity, email, devices, apps, and data. Defender for Cloud secure score measures cloud infrastructure posture against security controls and benchmarks across Azure, AWS, and GCP.\n\n- **B** is too narrow for both.\n- **C** reverses the coverage.\n- **D** is false; they are separate calculations.\n\n**Exam tip:** Microsoft 365 posture equals Microsoft Secure Score; cloud infrastructure posture equals Defender for Cloud secure score.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(116, 'solutions', 'Defender for Office 365 vs Cloud Apps',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes Defender for Office 365 from Defender for Cloud Apps?',
  ['Defender for Office 365 protects email and collaboration content; Defender for Cloud Apps governs SaaS application use', 'Defender for Office 365 governs SaaS apps; Defender for Cloud Apps protects email', 'Both protect endpoint devices', 'Both are compliance products'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Office 365 covers Exchange Online, Teams, SharePoint, and OneDrive content: phishing, spam, malicious links and attachments. Defender for Cloud Apps covers the SaaS estate: discovery of shadow IT, API-based governance, session controls, and anomaly detection.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is Defender for Endpoint.\n- **D** is Purview.\n\n**Exam tip:** email content equals Office 365; SaaS usage equals Cloud Apps.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(117, 'solutions', 'Defender for Endpoint vs Defender for Cloud',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes Defender for Endpoint from Defender for Cloud?',
  ['Defender for Endpoint protects devices; Defender for Cloud protects cloud infrastructure and workloads', 'Defender for Endpoint protects Azure subscriptions; Defender for Cloud protects laptops', 'Both protect only email', 'Both are identity governance products'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Endpoint runs on endpoints - laptops, servers, and mobile devices - with antivirus, EDR, and vulnerability management. Defender for Cloud assesses and protects cloud resources such as virtual machines, storage, databases, containers, and app services.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Entra ID Governance.\n\n**Exam tip:** device agent equals Endpoint; cloud resource posture and workload protection equals Defender for Cloud.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(118, 'solutions', 'Analytics rule vs playbook',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes a Sentinel analytics rule from a Sentinel playbook?',
  ['An analytics rule detects activity and creates an incident; a playbook automates the response', 'A playbook detects activity; an analytics rule automates the response', 'Both only visualise data in dashboards', 'Both are data ingestion connectors'],
  ['A'],
  'Analytics rules are detection logic: they query data and raise alerts or incidents. Playbooks are Logic Apps workflows that act on an incident - enrich, notify, approve, or remediate.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** describes workbooks.\n- **D** describes data connectors.\n\n**Exam tip:** detect equals analytics rule; act equals playbook.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(119, 'solutions', 'XDR vs Sentinel',
  'A company must investigate attacks across Microsoft 365 and must also correlate logs from an on-premises SIEM and a third-party firewall. Which solution combination is appropriate?',
  ['Defender XDR for Microsoft incidents and Sentinel for cross-platform correlation', 'Sentinel only, because it replaces Defender XDR', 'Defender XDR only, because it accepts third-party firewall logs', 'Defender for Cloud Apps for both requirements'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender XDR is strongest inside the Microsoft security estate and produces correlated incidents automatically. Sentinel accepts those incidents plus infrastructure and third-party logs, so it can correlate the full picture. They are complementary, not competing.\n\n- **B** is false; Sentinel does not replace XDR detections.\n- **C** is false; XDR does not ingest arbitrary third-party firewall logs.\n- **D** is the wrong product.\n\n**Exam tip:** Microsoft estate equals XDR; whole estate equals Sentinel.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions', { diagram: 'security-stack' }),
Q(120, 'solutions', 'Defender for Identity vs Cloud Apps',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes Defender for Identity from Defender for Cloud Apps?',
  ['Defender for Identity monitors Active Directory authentication; Defender for Cloud Apps monitors SaaS application activity', 'Defender for Identity monitors SaaS apps; Defender for Cloud Apps monitors Active Directory', 'Both monitor email attachments', 'Both protect virtual machines'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Identity sensors watch domain controllers for directory-based attacks. Defender for Cloud Apps connects to SaaS providers and network logs to find shadow IT, govern apps, and control sessions. Different assets, different signals, same XDR incident stream.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is Defender for Office 365.\n- **D** is Defender for Cloud.\n\n**Exam tip:** directory equals Identity; SaaS equals Cloud Apps.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(121, 'solutions', 'Sentinel - scenario',
  'A company needs one incident queue that includes Microsoft 365 alerts, Azure activity, and events from a third-party firewall. Which solution should be used?',
  ['Microsoft Sentinel', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Defender for Endpoint', 'Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager'],
  ['A'],
  'Only Sentinel is designed to ingest arbitrary Microsoft and non-Microsoft sources through data connectors and correlate them with custom analytics. The Defender products contribute their own signals but do not replace a cross-platform SIEM.\n\n- **B** covers SaaS only.\n- **C** covers devices only.\n- **D** tracks compliance assessments.\n\n**Exam tip:** mixed sources plus one queue equals Sentinel.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Sentinel'),
Q(122, 'solutions', 'Defender XDR components',
  'Which two Microsoft products feed signals into Microsoft Defender XDR? (Select two.)',
  ['Microsoft Defender for Identity', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Sentinel', 'Microsoft Purview Records Management'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Defender XDR natively correlates Defender for Endpoint, Defender for Identity, Defender for Office 365, and Defender for Cloud Apps, together with Microsoft Entra ID Protection signals.\n\n- **C** consumes XDR incidents as an upstream SIEM; it does not feed XDR.\n- **D** is a compliance capability.\n\n**Exam tip:** the four Defender workload products are the XDR family; Sentinel sits above them.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Describe Microsoft Defender XDR'),
Q(123, 'solutions', 'Service recognition - combined',
  'A hospital must reduce phishing email reaching clinicians and detect attackers moving through its on-premises Active Directory. Which two products should be deployed? (Select two.)',
  ['Microsoft Defender for Office 365', 'Microsoft Defender for Identity', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Phishing is an email threat, handled by Defender for Office 365 with anti-phishing, Safe Links, and Safe Attachments. Active Directory attack detection is the role of Defender for Identity sensors on domain controllers.\n\n- **C** governs SaaS applications.\n- **D** protects cloud infrastructure.\n\n**Exam tip:** match the asset in the scenario to the product name before reading the options.',
  'Describe Microsoft security solutions - Compare security solutions'),
Q(124, 'compliance', 'Purview - overview',
  'What is Microsoft Purview?',
  ['A family of data governance, protection, and compliance solutions for an organisation data estate', 'A cloud-native SIEM and SOAR platform', 'An endpoint detection and response product', 'A network security service'],
  ['A'],
  'Microsoft Purview brings together data governance (Data Map, Data Catalogue) and compliance (sensitivity labels, DLP, retention, insider risk, eDiscovery, audit, communication compliance, Compliance Manager). It helps an organisation know what data it has, protect it, and prove compliance.\n\n- **B** is Microsoft Sentinel.\n- **C** is Defender for Endpoint.\n- **D** is Azure network security.\n\n**Exam tip:** Purview equals data governance plus compliance, not threat detection.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Microsoft Purview'),
Q(125, 'compliance', 'Purview - Data Map',
  'Which Purview capability scans and catalogues data across Microsoft 365, Azure, on-premises, and multicloud sources?',
  ['Data Map', 'Data Loss Prevention', 'Audit', 'Communication compliance'],
  ['A'],
  'Data Map is the metadata backbone. Scans classify assets and record where data lives, how it moves, and what it contains, creating the inventory that the catalogue, DLP, and eDiscovery rely on.\n\n- **B** enforces sharing rules.\n- **C** records activity.\n- **D** reviews messages.\n\n**Exam tip:** discovery and classification of the estate equals Data Map.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Microsoft Purview'),
Q(126, 'compliance', 'Purview - Data Catalogue',
  'A data analyst needs a searchable inventory of data assets with business glossary terms and ownership. Which Purview capability should be used?',
  ['Data Catalogue', 'Insider Risk Management', 'Records Management', 'eDiscovery Premium'],
  ['A'],
  'Data Catalogue is the searchable view over Data Map metadata. Stewards can find assets, see classifications and lineage, and attach glossary terms and ownership so business users understand the data.\n\n- **B** detects insider risk.\n- **C** manages retention.\n- **D** supports legal investigations.\n\n**Exam tip:** Data Map is the index; Data Catalogue is how people browse it.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Microsoft Purview'),
Q(127, 'compliance', 'Sensitive information types',
  'Which Purview component recognises patterns such as credit-card numbers and national insurance numbers in content?',
  ['Sensitive information types', 'Retention labels', 'Access packages', 'Attack surface reduction rules'],
  ['A'],
  'Sensitive information types (SITs) are pattern definitions - regular expressions, keywords, checksums, and confidence levels - for categories of sensitive data. DLP policies, auto-labelling, and eDiscovery all use them to identify content.\n\n- **B** governs retention.\n- **C** governs access.\n- **D** hardens endpoints.\n\n**Exam tip:** SITs are the detectors underneath labels and DLP.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Microsoft Purview'),
Q(128, 'compliance', 'Purview - estate coverage',
  'Which two sources can Microsoft Purview Data Map scan? (Select two.)',
  ['Azure Blob Storage', 'On-premises SQL Server databases', 'Printed paper files in a locked cabinet', 'Verbal conversations in a meeting room'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Data Map supports connectors for Microsoft 365, Azure services, on-premises databases and file shares, and other clouds such as AWS S3. Scans extract metadata and classifications into the catalogue.\n\n- **C** and **D** have no digital connector and must be handled by policy and training.\n\n**Exam tip:** if it is stored digitally in a supported source, Purview can scan it.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Microsoft Purview'),
Q(129, 'compliance', 'Sensitivity labels - purpose',
  'What is the primary purpose of a sensitivity label in Microsoft Purview?',
  ['To classify content and apply protection such as encryption and content marking that travels with the item', 'To delete content after a fixed period', 'To block all external email', 'To record administrator activity in a log'],
  ['A'],
  'A sensitivity label describes the confidentiality of an item - for example Confidential or Highly Confidential - and can enforce encryption, content marking, and sharing restrictions. The label is stored with the file, so protection persists when the file leaves the organisation.\n\n- **B** is retention.\n- **C** is a DLP or transport rule action.\n- **D** is audit.\n\n**Exam tip:** a label classifies and protects; it does not control how long content is kept.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe sensitivity labels', { diagram: 'information-protection' }),
Q(130, 'compliance', 'Sensitivity labels - encryption',
  'A labelled document is emailed to a partner. The partner can open it, but cannot forward or print it. Which label setting achieves this?',
  ['Encryption with usage rights for specific users or groups', 'A retention label that keeps the item for five years', 'A DLP policy tip', 'A communication compliance policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Sensitivity label encryption uses Azure Rights Management to attach usage rights - view, edit, print, copy, forward - to the content. The rights travel with the file and are enforced even outside the tenant.\n\n- **B** controls retention, not permissions.\n- **C** educates the sender.\n- **D** reviews messages after the fact.\n\n**Exam tip:** rights that follow the file equal label-based encryption.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe sensitivity labels'),
Q(131, 'compliance', 'Sensitivity labels - content marking',
  'A label must add the word Confidential to the header of every Word document and a watermark to PowerPoint slides. Which label setting is used?',
  ['Content marking', 'Encryption', 'Auto-labelling', 'Retention'],
  ['A'],
  'Content marking adds a header, footer, or watermark to supported Office files and email when the label is applied. It is a visual deterrent and a reminder, not an access control.\n\n- **B** enforces permissions.\n- **C** chooses the label automatically.\n- **D** controls lifetime.\n\n**Exam tip:** header, footer, and watermark equal content marking.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe sensitivity labels'),
Q(132, 'compliance', 'Sensitivity labels - label policy',
  'How does an organisation make a sensitivity label available to users in Outlook, Word, and Teams?',
  ['Publish it in a label policy scoped to users or groups', 'Create a retention policy', 'Add the user to a security group', 'Enable audit logging'],
  ['A'],
  'Labels are created in the labelling taxonomy and then published through one or more label policies. A policy defines who sees the labels, which default label applies, whether justification is required, and whether a mandatory label is enforced.\n\n- **B** handles retention.\n- **C** grants resource access.\n- **D** records activity.\n\n**Exam tip:** a label does nothing until a label policy publishes it.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe sensitivity labels'),
Q(133, 'compliance', 'Sensitivity labels - auto-labelling',
  'A company wants files containing 10 or more credit-card numbers labelled Confidential automatically without user action. Which capability should be used?',
  ['Auto-labelling policies using sensitive information types', 'Manual labelling only', 'A retention policy', 'An access review'],
  ['A'],
  'Auto-labelling policies inspect content using sensitive information types, trainable classifiers, or exact data match and apply or recommend a label. Service-side labelling works in SharePoint, OneDrive, Exchange, and Teams; client-side labelling works in Office apps.\n\n- **B** depends on users.\n- **C** controls retention.\n- **D** recertifies access.\n\n**Exam tip:** content inspection plus automatic label equals an auto-labelling policy.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe sensitivity labels'),
Q(134, 'compliance', 'Sensitivity labels - priority',
  'Two sensitivity labels could apply to the same file. How is the label that wins determined?',
  ['By label priority order, where a lower order number has higher priority', 'By alphabetical order of the label names', 'By the label with the shortest name', 'By the user who created the file'],
  ['A'],
  'Labels are ordered in the labelling taxonomy. When content matches more than one auto-label rule, the label with the highest priority - the lowest order number - is applied. Sublabels sit under their parent and inherit its priority position.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are not how priority works.\n\n**Exam tip:** lower order number equals higher priority.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe sensitivity labels'),
Q(135, 'compliance', 'DLP - purpose',
  'An organisation must stop employees emailing credit-card numbers to external addresses. Which Microsoft solution should be used?',
  ['Data Loss Prevention policies', 'Retention labels', 'Insider Risk Management', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps'],
  ['A'],
  'DLP policies detect sensitive information types in Exchange, SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams, and on endpoints, then block, warn, or audit the action. This is prevention at the point of sharing rather than classification alone.\n\n- **B** controls retention.\n- **C** investigates risky user behaviour.\n- **D** governs SaaS usage.\n\n**Exam tip:** stop the sharing equals DLP; classify the item equals a sensitivity label.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Data Loss Prevention'),
Q(136, 'compliance', 'DLP - locations',
  'Which workloads can a Microsoft Purview DLP policy protect? (Select two.)',
  ['Exchange Online email', 'SharePoint and OneDrive sites', 'Azure virtual network subnets', 'Domain controller system files'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'DLP covers Microsoft 365 locations - Exchange, SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams chat and channel messages - and can extend to Windows and macOS endpoints through endpoint DLP, plus some non-Microsoft cloud apps through Defender for Cloud Apps.\n\n- **C** is network configuration, not content.\n- **D** is operating system files.\n\n**Exam tip:** DLP protects content where people collaborate and share.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Data Loss Prevention'),
Q(137, 'compliance', 'DLP - policy tips',
  'A user tries to email a document containing a national insurance number. The policy allows a justified override. What does the user see?',
  ['A policy tip explaining the rule with an option to override and provide a business justification', 'The message is silently deleted', 'The user account is disabled', 'A retention label is applied'],
  ['A'],
  'Policy tips educate users at the moment of risk: they explain which sensitive information triggered the rule and offer actions such as remove, override with justification, or report a false positive. Overrides are recorded for review.\n\n- **B** is a block without explanation.\n- **C** is disproportionate.\n- **D** is a labelling action.\n\n**Exam tip:** policy tips are the user-education half of DLP.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Data Loss Prevention'),
Q(138, 'compliance', 'DLP - endpoint',
  'A company must stop staff copying confidential files to personal USB drives. Which capability should be configured?',
  ['Endpoint DLP', 'Safe Attachments', 'Retention labels', 'Access reviews'],
  ['A'],
  'Endpoint DLP extends DLP to Windows and macOS devices, monitoring and controlling actions such as copy to removable storage, print, upload to cloud services, and copy to clipboard for sensitive items.\n\n- **B** is email detonation.\n- **C** is retention.\n- **D** is access governance.\n\n**Exam tip:** USB, print, and clipboard controls equal endpoint DLP.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Data Loss Prevention'),
Q(139, 'compliance', 'DLP - alerts',
  'After a DLP policy blocks a file share, the compliance team needs to see who triggered it and what action was taken. Where should they look?',
  ['DLP alerts and activity explorer', 'The Secure Score dashboard', 'A Sentinel workbook only', 'The label policy report'],
  ['A'],
  'DLP alerts record rule matches with the user, location, sensitive information type, and action taken. Activity explorer adds detail about label and DLP activity over time for investigation and tuning.\n\n- **B** is posture scoring.\n- **C** is optional and not the primary DLP record.\n- **D** covers label policy deployment.\n\n**Exam tip:** DLP alert history equals the alert and activity explorer views.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Data Loss Prevention'),
Q(140, 'compliance', 'DLP - actions',
  'Which two actions can a DLP policy take when it detects sensitive information? (Select two.)',
  ['Block the sharing action', 'Show a policy tip and allow an override with justification', 'Encrypt the entire disk', 'Delete the user account'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'DLP responses are proportional: audit only, show a policy tip, block with or without override, or restrict access for non-Microsoft apps. The right response depends on the sensitivity and the business process.\n\n- **C** is BitLocker, not DLP.\n- **D** is an administrative action, not a policy outcome.\n\n**Exam tip:** DLP can educate, block, or audit - usually starting with audit and tips.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Data Loss Prevention'),
Q(141, 'compliance', 'Retention policies',
  'A company must keep all Exchange mail for seven years and then delete it, regardless of which mailbox it is in. Which capability should be used?',
  ['A retention policy scoped to Exchange mailboxes', 'A sensitivity label on each message', 'A DLP policy', 'An eDiscovery hold'],
  ['A'],
  'Retention policies apply at the location level - Exchange mailboxes, SharePoint sites, OneDrive accounts, Teams messages - and contain rules to retain, delete, or retain then delete content for a period. They cover new and existing items without per-item work.\n\n- **B** is item-level and depends on labelling.\n- **C** blocks sharing.\n- **D** preserves content for a legal case.\n\n**Exam tip:** whole-location retention equals a retention policy.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe retention', { diagram: 'compliance-lifecycle' }),
Q(142, 'compliance', 'Retention labels',
  'A contract must be kept for ten years and then reviewed by legal before deletion. Which capability supports this?',
  ['A retention label with a disposition review', 'A retention policy with automatic deletion', 'A sensitivity label', 'A DLP policy'],
  ['A'],
  'Retention labels are applied to individual items manually, automatically by rule, or by a default policy. They can start retention from when the item was created, modified, or labelled, and can end with disposition review so a person confirms deletion.\n\n- **B** cannot require review before deletion.\n- **C** classifies and protects but does not set lifetime.\n- **D** prevents sharing.\n\n**Exam tip:** item-level retention with review equals a retention label.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe retention'),
Q(143, 'compliance', 'Retention policy vs label',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes a retention policy from a retention label?',
  ['A policy applies to a whole location; a label is applied to individual items and travels with them', 'A label applies to a whole location; a policy is applied per item', 'Both only delete content immediately', 'Both are sensitivity labels'],
  ['A'],
  'A retention policy is location-scoped and applies uniformly to everything in that mailbox or site. A retention label is item-scoped: it follows the document or email wherever it goes, and can trigger disposition review or be treated as a record.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is false; both can retain before deleting.\n- **D** is false; sensitivity and retention are separate label types.\n\n**Exam tip:** policy equals place; label equals item.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe retention'),
Q(144, 'compliance', 'Records management - record declaration',
  'A regulator requires that a signed policy document cannot be edited or deleted before its retention period ends. Which capability should be used?',
  ['Declare the item a record with a retention label', 'Apply a sensitivity label only', 'Place the item in a Teams channel', 'Enable audit logging'],
  ['A'],
  'Declaring an item a record locks down its content: editing is blocked, deletion is prevented, and the label can be changed only by authorised users. A regulatory record is even stricter and removes the ability to change or remove the label.\n\n- **B** classifies but does not make the item immutable.\n- **C** does not prevent edits.\n- **D** records activity but does not block changes.\n\n**Exam tip:** immutability equals record declaration.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe records management'),
Q(145, 'compliance', 'Records management - disposition review',
  'At the end of a retention period, a reviewer must confirm whether content should be deleted or retained longer. Which feature provides this checkpoint?',
  ['Disposition review', 'A DLP policy tip', 'An access review', 'Auto-labelling'],
  ['A'],
  'Disposition review creates a queue for designated reviewers when retention ends. They can approve deletion, extend retention with a new period, or relabel the item, and every decision is audited.\n\n- **B** is a user prompt at share time.\n- **C** recertifies access.\n- **D** applies labels automatically.\n\n**Exam tip:** human sign-off before deletion equals disposition review.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe records management'),
Q(146, 'compliance', 'Retention - capabilities',
  'Which two outcomes can retention settings produce? (Select two.)',
  ['Retain content for a defined period', 'Delete content at the end of a period', 'Encrypt content with usage rights', 'Block content from being emailed externally'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Retention is about time: keep content for a period, delete it after a period, or keep then delete. Retention labels can also trigger disposition review or declare a record.\n\n- **C** is sensitivity label encryption.\n- **D** is DLP.\n\n**Exam tip:** retention controls lifetime; labels and DLP control protection and sharing.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe retention'),
Q(147, 'compliance', 'Insider risk - purpose',
  'A company suspects an employee is downloading customer data before resigning. Which Purview solution should be used?',
  ['Insider Risk Management', 'Communication compliance', 'Records Management', 'Compliance Manager'],
  ['A'],
  'Insider Risk Management correlates signals such as file downloads, USB activity, print jobs, and anomalous access with HR events such as a resignation, then raises alerts and supports investigation while protecting user privacy.\n\n- **B** reviews message content for policy breaches.\n- **C** manages retention.\n- **D** tracks regulatory assessments.\n\n**Exam tip:** departing employee plus data theft equals Insider Risk Management.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Insider Risk Management'),
Q(148, 'compliance', 'Insider risk - policy templates',
  'Which template should be chosen to detect data theft by employees who have resigned?',
  ['Departing employee data theft', 'Security policy violation', 'Patient records management', 'Regulatory compliance assessment'],
  ['A'],
  'Insider Risk Management provides templates for data theft by departing users, general data leaks, leaks by priority users, security policy violations, and patient data misuse. The template selects the relevant indicators and thresholds.\n\n- **B** covers non-compliance with security policy.\n- **C** is a healthcare-specific misuse template, not the departing-employee case.\n- **D** is Compliance Manager.\n\n**Exam tip:** choose the template that matches the risk scenario, then tune indicators.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Insider Risk Management'),
Q(149, 'compliance', 'Insider risk - alerts',
  'An insider risk policy raises an alert. What should the compliance analyst do first?',
  ['Triage the alert, review the activity sequence, and decide whether to create a case', 'Disable the user account immediately', 'Delete the alert to reduce noise', 'Publish a new sensitivity label'],
  ['A'],
  'Alerts are triaged against the policy indicators and the user activity sequence. Confirmed risk can be promoted to a case for evidence collection, collaboration with legal or HR, and appropriate action, with privacy controls such as anonymised usernames available.\n\n- **B** is premature and may be unjustified.\n- **C** destroys evidence.\n- **D** is unrelated to the alert.\n\n**Exam tip:** alert, triage, case, investigate - in that order.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Insider Risk Management'),
Q(150, 'compliance', 'Insider risk - privacy',
  'Which feature helps protect employee privacy during an insider risk investigation?',
  ['Pseudonymising usernames so reviewers see anonymised identifiers', 'Publishing all alerts to the whole company', 'Disabling audit logging', 'Deleting all HR records'],
  ['A'],
  'Insider Risk Management supports privacy by design: role-based access, pseudonymised user names in alerts, and audit of reviewer actions. Identities can be revealed only by an authorised role when an investigation requires it.\n\n- **B** would violate privacy.\n- **C** removes accountability.\n- **D** destroys evidence.\n\n**Exam tip:** privacy by design means reviewers see behaviour before identity.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Insider Risk Management'),
Q(151, 'compliance', 'eDiscovery - purpose',
  'What is the purpose of Microsoft Purview eDiscovery?',
  ['To identify, preserve, collect, review, and export content for legal or investigation requests', 'To block sensitive files from being emailed', 'To retain all content for a fixed period', 'To score the tenant against a regulatory standard'],
  ['A'],
  'eDiscovery supports the electronic discovery process: search across mailboxes, sites, and Teams, place content on hold so it cannot be deleted, collect it into a review set, filter and review it, then export for legal proceedings.\n\n- **B** is DLP.\n- **C** is retention.\n- **D** is Compliance Manager.\n\n**Exam tip:** legal case or investigation equals eDiscovery.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe eDiscovery'),
Q(152, 'compliance', 'eDiscovery - Standard vs Premium',
  'Which capability is available only in eDiscovery Premium?',
  ['Custodian management with legal hold notifications and review-set analytics', 'Keyword search across mailboxes', 'Export of search results', 'Case creation'],
  ['A'],
  'eDiscovery Standard covers search, hold, and export. Premium adds custodian and non-custodian data sources, hold notifications and acknowledgements, review sets with analytics such as near-duplicate detection and threading, predictive coding, and better remediation.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are available in Standard.\n\n**Exam tip:** custodians, notifications, and review analytics are the Premium markers.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe eDiscovery'),
Q(153, 'compliance', 'eDiscovery - holds',
  'During litigation, content must not be deleted even if a user empties their deleted items. Which eDiscovery action achieves this?',
  ['Place the content sources on an eDiscovery hold', 'Apply a sensitivity label', 'Create a DLP policy', 'Run an audit log search'],
  ['A'],
  'An eDiscovery hold preserves content in place across the specified mailboxes and sites, independent of retention settings or user deletion. Items remain discoverable for the case until the hold is released.\n\n- **B** classifies content.\n- **C** prevents sharing.\n- **D** only reports activity.\n\n**Exam tip:** litigation preservation equals an eDiscovery hold.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe eDiscovery'),
Q(154, 'compliance', 'eDiscovery - review sets',
  'After a search collects thousands of items, the legal team needs to filter, tag, and de-duplicate them. Where does this happen?',
  ['In a review set', 'In the Data Catalogue', 'In a retention policy', 'In the audit log'],
  ['A'],
  'A review set is a container of collected content. Reviewers can filter by date, sender, keywords, and metadata, run analytics to group near-duplicates and email threads, apply tags, and then export the relevant subset.\n\n- **B** catalogues data assets.\n- **C** governs retention.\n- **D** records activity.\n\n**Exam tip:** collection goes to a review set; analysis and tagging happen there.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe eDiscovery'),
Q(155, 'compliance', 'eDiscovery - workflow',
  'Which sequence describes a typical eDiscovery workflow?',
  ['Identify and search, preserve with a hold, collect into a review set, review, then export', 'Export, delete, search, then hold', 'Label, encrypt, retain, then delete', 'Assess, score, remediate, then certify'],
  ['A'],
  'The defensible workflow starts with identifying sources and searching, then preserving relevant content before it can be lost, collecting it for review, filtering and tagging it, and exporting only what is needed.\n\n- **B** destroys evidence and reverses the order.\n- **C** is information protection and retention.\n- **D** is Compliance Manager.\n\n**Exam tip:** preserve before collect - deletion risk comes first.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe eDiscovery'),
Q(156, 'compliance', 'Audit - purpose',
  'What does Microsoft Purview Audit record?',
  ['User and administrator activity across Microsoft 365 services', 'The sensitivity level of each document', 'The network topology of Azure', 'The licence count for each subscription'],
  ['A'],
  'Audit captures events such as sign-ins, file access, mailbox permission changes, label changes, and administrative actions, with who, what, when, and where. Investigators search these records to reconstruct what happened.\n\n- **B** is a label property.\n- **C** is network documentation.\n- **D** is licensing.\n\n**Exam tip:** who did what, when equals audit.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Audit'),
Q(157, 'compliance', 'Audit - Standard vs Premium',
  'Which capability is provided by Audit Premium but not Audit Standard?',
  ['Retention policies that keep audit records for up to 10 years and high-value events such as MailItemsAccessed', 'Searching audit records by date', 'Exporting audit records to CSV', 'Recording administrator actions'],
  ['A'],
  'Audit Standard retains records for 180 days and covers the core event set. Premium adds one-year default retention, custom audit retention policies up to 10 years, high-value events needed for investigations, and higher API bandwidth.\n\n- **B**, **C**, and **D** are available in Standard.\n\n**Exam tip:** long retention and high-value events are the Premium markers.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Audit'),
Q(158, 'compliance', 'Audit - retention',
  'How long does Audit Standard retain audit records by default?',
  ['180 days', '30 days', '7 years', '10 years'],
  ['A'],
  'Audit Standard keeps records for 180 days. Audit Premium keeps them for one year by default and allows retention policies of up to 10 years for users who need long-term investigative history.\n\n- **B** is too short for the default.\n- **C** and **D** require Premium retention policies.\n\n**Exam tip:** 180 days Standard, 1 year Premium default, up to 10 years with a policy.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Audit'),
Q(159, 'compliance', 'Audit - investigation',
  'An investigator needs to find every time a specific administrator changed mailbox permissions last month. Which tool should be used?',
  ['Audit log search in Microsoft Purview', 'Secure Score', 'A retention label', 'Cloud Discovery'],
  ['A'],
  'Audit log search filters on date range, user, activity, and record type. Searching for mailbox permission activities returns each change with the administrator, timestamp, target mailbox, and result.\n\n- **B** measures posture.\n- **C** governs retention.\n- **D** discovers SaaS.\n\n**Exam tip:** reconstructing past actions equals an audit log search.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Audit'),
Q(160, 'compliance', 'Audit - administrator activity',
  'A compliance officer must prove which administrator granted a user access to a sensitive site. Which record type provides this?',
  ['Audit records for site permission changes', 'Sensitivity label analytics', 'A DLP policy tip', 'A retention disposition review'],
  ['A'],
  'Audit records capture site collection and permission changes, including who made the change and when. This evidence supports both internal investigations and regulatory reporting.\n\n- **B** reports label usage.\n- **C** is a share-time prompt.\n- **D** is a deletion decision.\n\n**Exam tip:** permission changes are audit events, not label events.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Audit'),
Q(161, 'compliance', 'Communication compliance - purpose',
  'An organisation must detect harassing or inappropriate messages in Teams and Exchange. Which Purview solution should be used?',
  ['Communication compliance', 'Records Management', 'Compliance Manager', 'Data Map'],
  ['A'],
  'Communication compliance uses policies with classifiers, sensitive information types, and keyword lists to detect policy violations in email, Teams, Viva Engage, and other channels, then routes matches to reviewers for action.\n\n- **B** manages retention.\n- **C** tracks regulatory assessments.\n- **D** catalogues data.\n\n**Exam tip:** review what people said equals communication compliance.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe communication compliance'),
Q(162, 'compliance', 'Communication compliance - policies',
  'Which components can a communication compliance policy use to identify problematic messages? (Select two.)',
  ['Trainable classifiers', 'Sensitive information types', 'Retention labels', 'Access packages'],
  ['A', 'B'],
  'Policies combine detection methods: trainable classifiers for categories such as harassment or threats, sensitive information types for data such as card numbers, and keyword or custom lists, plus direction and user scope.\n\n- **C** governs retention.\n- **D** governs access.\n\n**Exam tip:** communication compliance detects language and content, not lifetime or permissions.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe communication compliance'),
Q(163, 'compliance', 'Communication compliance vs DLP',
  'How does communication compliance differ from DLP?',
  ['Communication compliance reviews messages after they are sent for policy violations; DLP prevents or warns about sharing sensitive data in real time', 'DLP reviews messages after they are sent; communication compliance blocks sharing in real time', 'Both only apply to SharePoint', 'Both are retention features'],
  ['A'],
  'DLP is preventive: it evaluates an action such as sending or sharing and can block it. Communication compliance is detective and review-oriented: it examines communications for conduct, harassment, or regulatory breaches and sends matches to human reviewers.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is false; both cover multiple workloads.\n- **D** is false.\n\n**Exam tip:** prevent data sharing equals DLP; review conduct equals communication compliance.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe communication compliance'),
Q(164, 'compliance', 'Compliance Manager - purpose',
  'What does Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager help an organisation do?',
  ['Assess, track, and improve compliance against regulations and standards', 'Detect malware on endpoints', 'Discover shadow IT applications', 'Manage privileged administrator roles'],
  ['A'],
  'Compliance Manager provides assessments built from templates for standards such as ISO 27001, GDPR, HIPAA, and NIST. It maps controls to Microsoft and customer actions, tracks implementation, and calculates a compliance score.\n\n- **B** is Defender for Endpoint.\n- **C** is Defender for Cloud Apps.\n- **D** is PIM.\n\n**Exam tip:** regulatory assessment and improvement tracking equals Compliance Manager.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Compliance Manager'),
Q(165, 'compliance', 'Compliance Manager - assessments',
  'A bank must measure itself against PCI DSS. What should it create in Compliance Manager?',
  ['An assessment based on the PCI DSS template', 'A retention policy', 'A sensitivity label', 'An eDiscovery case'],
  ['A'],
  'An assessment groups the controls for one standard or regulation, using a Microsoft-provided template or a custom one. It shows which controls are met, which need work, and which are the customer responsibility.\n\n- **B** controls retention.\n- **C** classifies content.\n- **D** supports legal discovery.\n\n**Exam tip:** one standard equals one assessment.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Compliance Manager'),
Q(166, 'compliance', 'Compliance Manager - score',
  'What does the compliance score in Compliance Manager represent?',
  ['The proportion of assessed controls and improvement actions that have been implemented', 'The percentage of users who completed security training', 'The number of incidents closed this month', 'The uptime of Microsoft 365'],
  ['A'],
  'The score is calculated from the points achieved against the points available for the assessed controls. Microsoft-managed controls count automatically; customer actions must be implemented and marked as tested or implemented.\n\n- **B** is training completion, not compliance posture.\n- **C** is incident metrics.\n- **D** is availability.\n\n**Exam tip:** compliance score is a control-implementation percentage.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Compliance Manager'),
Q(167, 'compliance', 'Compliance Manager - improvement actions',
  'Compliance Manager shows a control is not met. What should the compliance team do next?',
  ['Implement the recommended improvement action and update its status', 'Delete the assessment', 'Disable audit logging', 'Ignore it because Microsoft handles all controls'],
  ['A'],
  'Each control links to improvement actions with guidance, affected services, and a status such as not assessed, in progress, implemented, or not applicable. Updating the status and evidence moves the score and creates an audit trail.\n\n- **B** loses the measurement.\n- **C** removes evidence.\n- **D** is false; customers own many controls.\n\n**Exam tip:** improvement actions are the work items behind the score.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Describe Compliance Manager'),
Q(168, 'compliance', 'Sensitivity labels vs DLP',
  'Which statement correctly distinguishes sensitivity labels from DLP policies?',
  ['Labels classify and protect an item persistently; DLP detects sensitive content and prevents or warns about sharing it', 'DLP classifies and encrypts items; labels block sharing in real time', 'Both only set retention periods', 'Both only apply to on-premises file servers'],
  ['A'],
  'A sensitivity label is metadata plus protection that travels with the item - encryption, marking, and sharing limits. DLP evaluates an action such as sending or uploading and can block it, warn the user, or audit it. Labels and DLP are usually deployed together.\n\n- **B** reverses them.\n- **C** is retention.\n- **D** is false; both are cloud-first.\n\n**Exam tip:** label equals classify and protect; DLP equals detect and prevent sharing.',
  'Describe Microsoft compliance solutions - Compare compliance solutions'),
Q(169, 'security', 'Case - Zero Trust',
  'Contoso Healthcare gives each clinician access only to the patient records for their own ward. Which Zero Trust principle does this apply?',
  ['Least privilege', 'Assume breach', 'Verify explicitly', 'Defence in depth'],
  ['A'],
  'Limiting each clinician to the records needed for their ward is least privilege: the minimum access required for the task, with no broad access to every patient record.\n\n- **B** is about designing for an existing compromise.\n- **C** authenticates and authorises each request, but does not describe the narrow scope of access.\n- **D** is a layered-controls strategy.\n\n**Exam tip:** scope of access equals least privilege.',
  'SC-900 case study 1 - Contoso Healthcare', { case: 'contoso-healthcare' }),
Q(170, 'solutions', 'Case - phishing protection',
  'Contoso wants to reduce the number of staff who fall for phishing email. Which Microsoft solution should be deployed?',
  ['Microsoft Defender for Office 365', 'Microsoft Defender for Endpoint', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Office 365 provides anti-phishing policies, Safe Links, Safe Attachments, and attack simulation training - the combination that detects phishing and improves user behaviour.\n\n- **B** protects devices from malware.\n- **C** governs SaaS applications.\n- **D** protects cloud infrastructure.\n\n**Exam tip:** phishing is an email threat, so start with Defender for Office 365.',
  'SC-900 case study 1 - Contoso Healthcare', { case: 'contoso-healthcare' }),
Q(171, 'identity', 'Case - privileged access',
  'Contoso administrators currently hold permanent Global Administrator roles. Which Entra capability should govern this access?',
  ['Privileged Identity Management with eligible, time-bound assignments', 'Self-service password reset', 'Dynamic group membership', 'Microsoft Entra Connect'],
  ['A'],
  'PIM removes standing Global Administrator access. Administrators activate the role when needed, with approval, MFA, and a justification, and every activation is audited.\n\n- **B** recovers user passwords.\n- **C** builds group membership by attribute.\n- **D** synchronises identities.\n\n**Exam tip:** standing admin rights equal a PIM conversation.',
  'SC-900 case study 1 - Contoso Healthcare', { case: 'contoso-healthcare' }),
Q(172, 'compliance', 'Case - patient record protection',
  'Contoso must ensure patient records stay encrypted and can be opened only by authorised clinical staff, even when the file is shared externally. Which solution should be used?',
  ['Sensitivity labels with encryption', 'Retention labels', 'Communication compliance', 'Compliance Manager'],
  ['A'],
  'Sensitivity labels classify content and can apply encryption with usage rights that travel with the file, so protection persists after it leaves the tenant. This is the information-protection control for patient records.\n\n- **B** controls lifetime.\n- **C** reviews messages.\n- **D** tracks assessments.\n\n**Exam tip:** encryption that follows the file equals a sensitivity label.',
  'SC-900 case study 1 - Contoso Healthcare', { case: 'contoso-healthcare', diagram: 'information-protection' }),
Q(173, 'compliance', 'Case - insider threat',
  'Contoso suspects an employee is exfiltrating patient data before leaving. Which Purview solution should be used to investigate?',
  ['Insider Risk Management', 'Records Management', 'Audit Standard only', 'Data Loss Prevention only'],
  ['A'],
  'Insider Risk Management correlates departure signals with downloads, USB activity, and unusual access, raises alerts, and supports a privacy-controlled investigation with cases and evidence.\n\n- **B** manages retention.\n- **C** records activity but does not analyse intent or sequence.\n- **D** blocks sharing but does not investigate behaviour.\n\n**Exam tip:** suspicious user behaviour over time equals Insider Risk Management.',
  'SC-900 case study 1 - Contoso Healthcare', { case: 'contoso-healthcare' }),
Q(174, 'solutions', 'Case - SIEM',
  'Contoso needs to centralise security analytics and investigate incidents across its whole estate. Which Microsoft solution should it deploy?',
  ['Microsoft Sentinel', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps', 'Microsoft Purview', 'Microsoft Defender for Endpoint'],
  ['A'],
  'Sentinel is the cloud-native SIEM and SOAR platform. It ingests Microsoft and third-party logs, applies analytics rules, and gives one incident queue with automation playbooks.\n\n- **B** governs SaaS.\n- **C** is the compliance suite.\n- **D** protects endpoints.\n\n**Exam tip:** central analytics across sources equals Sentinel.',
  'SC-900 case study 1 - Contoso Healthcare', { case: 'contoso-healthcare', diagram: 'security-stack' }),
Q(175, 'solutions', 'Case - credential attacks',
  'Fabrikam must detect credential theft and password-spray attacks against its domain accounts. Which Defender product should be used?',
  ['Microsoft Defender for Identity', 'Microsoft Defender for Endpoint', 'Microsoft Defender for Office 365', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender for Identity sensors on domain controllers analyse authentication traffic and detect credential theft, password spray, reconnaissance, and lateral movement.\n\n- **B** protects devices.\n- **C** protects email.\n- **D** protects cloud infrastructure.\n\n**Exam tip:** domain account attacks equal Defender for Identity.',
  'SC-900 case study 2 - Fabrikam Financial Services', { case: 'fabrikam-financial' }),
Q(176, 'solutions', 'Case - incident correlation',
  'Fabrikam currently investigates identity, email, endpoint, and cloud app alerts separately. Which solution gives one correlated incident view across those products?',
  ['Microsoft Defender XDR', 'Microsoft Sentinel only', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud', 'Microsoft Purview'],
  ['A'],
  'Defender XDR natively correlates signals from Defender for Identity, Office 365, Endpoint, and Cloud Apps into incidents with a shared investigation timeline and automated response.\n\n- **B** is a SIEM that can ingest many sources, but the native correlation of the Microsoft products is XDR.\n- **C** protects cloud workloads.\n- **D** is compliance.\n\n**Exam tip:** native cross-product correlation of the Defender family equals XDR.',
  'SC-900 case study 2 - Fabrikam Financial Services', { case: 'fabrikam-financial' }),
Q(177, 'compliance', 'Case - regulatory assessment',
  'Fabrikam must assess itself against banking regulations and track remediation. Which solution should be used?',
  ['Microsoft Purview Compliance Manager', 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud regulatory compliance only', 'Microsoft Sentinel', 'Microsoft Entra access reviews'],
  ['A'],
  'Compliance Manager provides assessments for standards and regulations, maps controls to improvement actions, and calculates a compliance score with an audit trail of implementation.\n\n- **B** maps technical controls for cloud resources but is not the organisation-wide assessment tool.\n- **C** is a SIEM.\n- **D** recertifies access.\n\n**Exam tip:** assessment against a standard plus improvement tracking equals Compliance Manager.',
  'SC-900 case study 2 - Fabrikam Financial Services', { case: 'fabrikam-financial' }),
Q(178, 'compliance', 'Case - document sharing',
  'Fabrikam must prevent staff emailing documents that contain customer account numbers to personal addresses. Which solution should be configured?',
  ['A Data Loss Prevention policy with a block action', 'A retention policy', 'Communication compliance', 'Records Management'],
  ['A'],
  'DLP detects sensitive information types such as account numbers in Exchange and blocks or warns at the point of sharing. Sensitivity labels classify and protect the document, but DLP is the control that stops the email leaving.\n\n- **B** controls retention.\n- **C** reviews messages after they are sent.\n- **D** manages records.\n\n**Exam tip:** stop the sharing action equals DLP.',
  'SC-900 case study 2 - Fabrikam Financial Services', { case: 'fabrikam-financial' }),
Q(179, 'compliance', 'Case - administrator audit',
  'Fabrikam must retain evidence of administrator activity for several years for regulators. Which solution should be used?',
  ['Microsoft Purview Audit Premium with a long-term retention policy', 'Audit Standard with default retention', 'A DLP policy', 'An access review'],
  ['A'],
  'Audit Premium keeps records for one year by default and supports retention policies of up to 10 years, which meets long regulatory evidence requirements. It also captures high-value events useful in investigations.\n\n- **B** retains for 180 days by default.\n- **C** prevents sharing.\n- **D** recertifies access.\n\n**Exam tip:** multi-year audit evidence equals Audit Premium retention policies.',
  'SC-900 case study 2 - Fabrikam Financial Services', { case: 'fabrikam-financial' }),
Q(180, 'identity', 'Case - guest access',
  'Fabrikam guest accounts accumulate and are never reviewed. Which Entra capability should be used to confirm external users still need access?',
  ['Access reviews', 'Privileged Identity Management', 'Self-service password reset', 'Microsoft Entra Connect'],
  ['A'],
  'Access reviews can target guest users and ask the resource owner, the guest, or a manager to confirm or remove access on a schedule. Cross-tenant access settings then control how those guests authenticate and what they may access.\n\n- **B** governs privileged roles.\n- **C** recovers passwords.\n- **D** synchronises identities.\n\n**Exam tip:** recurring guest recertification equals access reviews.',
  'SC-900 case study 2 - Fabrikam Financial Services', { case: 'fabrikam-financial' })
);
